# The interface between Sokar and anything with a user in front of it.
#
# This file is the contract, not a description of one: the daemon serves it verbatim through
# org.varlink.service.GetInterfaceDescription, and a test fails the build when a method is
# registered without appearing here or appears here without being registered. A client may
# therefore read it off a running daemon and trust that it matches.
#
# Everything is reached over one unix socket, owner-only, in Sokar's runtime directory
# ($XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/sokar/sokard.sock). There is no port and no authentication of its own: the
# filesystem decides who may connect, and a remote client forwards the socket over SSH rather
# than reaching a listener.
#
# Framing is varlink's own: one JSON object per call, NUL-separated, no length prefix. A call
# carrying "more": true asks for a stream, and every reply but the last carries
# "continues": true.
#
# A field declared ?type is genuinely absent, not empty. Everything else is always present -
# a string that has no value arrives as "", because an interface has to render something.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- compatibility
#
# NOT IN FORCE YET. Everything below describes what this interface promises once there is a
# release. There is none: the version is 0.1.0-SNAPSHOT, there is no release tag, and the only
# clients are the daemon and the frontend in this same organisation, changed together by the
# people writing them. Until that stops being true, a method here may be renamed, removed or
# given a different meaning in place, and the frontend is adapted in the same breath - rather
# than growing a Tasks2 to carry a mistake nobody ever depended on.
#
# What this exemption does NOT license: silently changing a method while a client still reads
# the old meaning. The rule it replaces is "keep the old shape"; the rule it does not replace is
# "both sides agree, deliberately, and the change is written down".
#
# It ends at the first release, and the decision to release is the decision to freeze this. From
# then on the interface only grows, exactly as described below, and a shape that turns out wrong
# costs a Tasks2 instead of an edit.
#
# A fleet is not upgraded at once, so a frontend has to work against a daemon older than
# itself. Three things make that possible, and a client should use all three.
#
# 1. The trailing 1 in the interface name is the promise. Within it this interface only ever
#    grows: new methods, new ?parameters, new reply fields. Nothing that is here will be
#    removed, renamed, given a different type, or made to mean something else. A change that
#    cannot be made that way becomes org.fuin.sokar.Tasks2, and a daemon serves both for at
#    least one release - so a client reads org.varlink.service.GetInfo, takes the highest
#    interface name it understands from the interfaces list, and uses that. Version numbers
#    are not compared to decide this.
#
# 2. Per feature, not per connection. Calling a method an older daemon does not have answers
#    org.varlink.service.MethodNotFound. That is a normal answer, not a fault: an interface
#    disables that one feature and keeps everything else. The same goes for a ?parameter an
#    older daemon ignores - it will behave as though it were not given, which is what its
#    default says it does.
#
# 3. Unknown values must not be fatal. New reply fields will appear in replies a client did
#    not expect, and new values will appear in enumerations it does not know - Outcome in
#    particular will gain entries. A client MUST ignore fields it does not recognize and MUST
#    render an unrecognized enumeration value rather than failing on it. This is part of the
#    contract: adding a value is not treated as a breaking change, so a client that cannot
#    survive one will break on a routine release.
#
# org.varlink.service.GetInfo also reports the daemon's own version, which is a build
# version and moves independently of the number in the interface name. Show it, log it, and
# put it in a bug report - but do not gate features on it. What a daemon can do is answered
# by 1 and 2 above, which stay true no matter how the product is numbered.
#
# org.varlink.service.GetInterfaceDescription returns this file, from the running daemon.
# A client that wants certainty rather than assumption can read the contract off the thing
# it is talking to.

interface org.fuin.sokar.Tasks1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- types

# What the work in a task is doing, as against what its container is doing.
#
# "running" answers the container's question. This answers the one an operator has, and they are
# not the same: a task blocked on a question nobody saw and a task grinding through a build are
# both running: true, and that difference is the one that costs an afternoon.
type Activity (
  # Its container is not up. Stopped, finished or dead - all three are gone.
  DEAD,
  # Waiting for a person to answer something, said by the thing that asked rather than guessed
  # from how long it has been quiet. waitingFor says what about.
  WAITING,
  # Producing output.
  WORKING,
  # Up, producing nothing, and not waiting for anybody as far as anything can tell.
  IDLE,
  # Up, and nothing on this side can see what it is doing - a task somebody attached a terminal
  # to writes its work to that terminal. Its own value rather than "idle", because a state that
  # is silently wrong is worse than one that says it does not know.
  UNKNOWN
)

# How a person is meant to be involved in a task.
type Mode (
  # A terminal in the container, driven by hand.
  SHELL,
  # The agent's own session, attached, with a person working through it.
  AGENT,
  # Started with a prompt and left to run. Nobody is expected to be watching.
  UNATTENDED
)

# What Start would do to one task, answered before anybody presses it.
#
# An interface names its action by this and shows a refusal as unavailable with its reason, rather
# than letting somebody find out by pressing. Answered per task in List and Watch rather than by a
# call per row: an interface drawing forty tiles would otherwise ask forty times for something the
# listing already knew.
#
# Distinct from StartOutcome, which answers "can work begin in this PROJECT at all" - agent,
# provider, credential. This answers "what happens to THIS task". A client MUST render a value it
# does not know rather than failing on it; this enumeration will gain entries.
type StartAction (
  # Nothing exists under this name. Start creates the container and starts it.
  CREATE,
  # It exists and is stopped. Start brings it back with the workspace, the branch and the
  # uncommitted changes it already has.
  RESUME,
  # It is already running. Start refuses rather than starting a second one; Attach is what
  # somebody wants.
  RUNNING,
  # It would be resumed, but the gate token it has to come back holding is in a vault that is
  # locked. The per-task face of StartOutcome's VAULT_LOCKED, and for the same reason: the one
  # thing that helps is unlocking, so an interface offers that instead of reporting a failure.
  NEEDS_VAULT,
  # Its name carries a run id from before one container per task, so nothing maps it to a task and
  # nothing starts it. Remove is what applies. 'startDetail' names the task it belonged to.
  SUPERSEDED_NAME,
  # It was started before this machine restarted. Its sockets were under the runtime directory,
  # which the system clears on restart, and the container is bound to one of them, so Start
  # refuses. The workspace is still inside the container: 'startDetail' says how to copy it out
  # before Remove.
  PREDATES_RESTART,
  # Something not specific to this task is in the way - project file, agent, provider or
  # credential. 'startDetail' carries the StartOutcome value naming which.
  NOT_READY
)

# One task on this machine, running or not.
#
# A task is a container plus the helpers Sokar started beside it. Both halves matter: a
# container that is up with no helpers has lost its gate or its clearance watcher, and is not
# the same thing as a healthy task.
# What an agent's screen says about waiting for a person. See Task.screen.
type ScreenState (WAITING, NOT_WAITING, UNDECLARED, UNSEEN)

# Whether a finished run's last message was a question. See Task.asked.
type Asked (ASKED, DID_NOT_ASK, CANNOT_SAY)

type Task (
  # Container name, which is what every other method takes as its task parameter.
  name: string,
  # Project the task belongs to, or "" when nothing recorded it.
  project: string,
  # Which of the project's repositories this task works on.
  #
  # "" for a container created before repositories existed. That is a task on the project's own
  # repository - the only one there was - and is to be rendered as such, NOT as "unknown".
  #
  # A task works on exactly one repository, fixed when the task was created. Carry it over when
  # offering to start a stopped task again, and pass it back as Start's 'repository'.
  repository: string,
  # The commit this task's configuration was verified at, or "" when nothing verified it.
  #
  # A task of a followed project runs against the file the machine checked, and that file moves on.
  # "What was this task actually running under" is asked after something went wrong, so it is kept
  # with the task rather than read back out of the project.
  commit: string,
  # offline, guarded or online - or "" when nothing recorded it.
  securityClass: string,
  # The container runtime's own words, such as "Up 4 minutes" or "Exited (143) 2 seconds ago".
  # Free text meant for a person; do not parse it. Use running for the decision.
  state: string,
  # Whether the runtime says it is up.
  running: bool,
  # How many recorded helper processes are alive.
  helpers: int,
  # Agent running in it, by the name Agents reports. "" when it has none, or when the task was
  # started by a Sokar that did not record it.
  agent: string,
  # How somebody is meant to be involved: SHELL, AGENT or UNATTENDED, spelled as Mode spells
  # them. A string rather than the Mode type because it has a fourth state the type does not: ""
  # for a task started before this field existed, where nothing recorded one and inventing an
  # answer would be worse than admitting there is none.
  mode: string,
  # What an unattended task was asked to do. Kept after it has finished, because "what was this
  # asked to do" is a question people have about a task that is over. "" for the other modes.
  prompt: string,
  # Ref its work goes to, such as refs/sokar/incoming/shell. "" when it has no gate.
  branch: string,
  # When the current state began, ISO-8601: a running task since it started, a stopped one since
  # it exited. "" when the runtime could not say - a container that was created and never started
  # has no honest answer. This is what "idle for forty minutes" is arithmetic on; state is not.
  since: string,
  # What the work is doing, beside state rather than instead of it.
  activity: Activity,
  # What it is waiting to be told, when activity is WAITING. "" otherwise.
  waitingFor: string,
  # What this task does with a blocked connection: "prompt", "allow", "deny" or "off" - the value
  # Start was given. "" when nothing recorded it, which is every task started before this field
  # existed.
  #
  # "off" is the one to mark wherever the task appears: nothing asks and nothing is refused, so
  # the firewall is loaded and no decision is ever put to anybody. It is a state somebody chose,
  # and an interface that shows it like any other run is hiding the choice.
  clearance: string,
  # A changeable caption somebody gave this task. "" when nobody has, which is every task by
  # default and is a normal state rather than a gap.
  #
  # BESIDE the name, never instead of it: 'name' is what every other method takes and what
  # somebody types into sokar at the machine, so a row that showed only this would make the
  # interface and the command line disagree about what a thing is called. Absent and empty are
  # deliberately the same state.
  label: string,
  # Whether this task's own work is waiting at the gate. 1 when its ref is waiting for review, 0
  # otherwise - and 0 for an online project, where nothing is ever reviewed, which is not a
  # smaller number but a question the class does not have.
  #
  # Answered here rather than left to a join, because there is no join to make: 'name' is a
  # CONTAINER name (sokar-<project>-<task>-<run>), PendingPush.name is a TASK name, and several
  # containers over time share one ref. Anything lined up from the two would be right for at most
  # one of them and silently wrong for the rest.
  waiting: int,
  # Name within the project, which is what Start takes. 'name' is the container it produced.
  #
  # Carried rather than derived: an interface must not cut a prefix off 'name' to get it, because
  # the two are related only by a rule Sokar owns and may change - it changed in this release. ""
  # for a container from before this field existed, where nothing recorded one.
  task: string,
  # What Start would do to this task, without doing it.
  startAction: StartAction,
  # Which refusal, when startAction is one: the StartOutcome value for NOT_READY, the task a
  # SUPERSEDED_NAME belonged to, free text otherwise. For RESUME, "" - or, for a task the
  # machine's restart took down, "the machine restarted; starting it brings it back whole", so an
  # interface can say why it is down without reading a boot log. "" otherwise.
  startDetail: string,
  # What this task is doing that takes minutes and has no other visible sign, such as "building".
  # "" when it is in none.
  #
  # Here rather than only in Start's stream, because a Start given 'now' returns at once and a
  # stream tied to the call that started it dies with that call. A window opened a minute later, or
  # a second machine watching the same daemon, would otherwise see a task with nothing behind it.
  # The output itself is read with Tail.
  phase: string,
  # Whether the agent's own screen shows it waiting for a person - DERIVED from a rule the agent's
  # package declares, read from the screen tmux draws in an attached task. Never observed, and kept
  # apart from activity and waitingFor, which are: render it as a reading, not a fact.
  #
  # WAITING      a declared rule matched
  # NOT_WAITING  the agent declares rules and none matched - quiet is not waiting
  # UNDECLARED   the agent declares nothing for its screen, so nothing can be said. Not NOT_WAITING:
  #              "this agent cannot tell us" is honest, "not waiting" would not be
  # UNSEEN       nothing here can see its screen: not attached, not running, or a pager is over it
  screen: ScreenState,
  # What it waits for, when the rule that matched says. "" otherwise.
  screenWaitingFor: string,
  # The agent declares rules, its screen has been read for a day, and none has ever matched: more
  # likely a declaration its current version no longer fits than an agent that never waits.
  screenUnproven: bool,
  # What its last unattended run said last, from the run's own records, where the agent declares
  # which record that is. Observed, not derived. "" when it does not say, or said nothing yet. The
  # run's next start replaces it.
  lastMessage: string,
  # Whether that last message put something to the person. DERIVED, and three-valued on purpose:
  # CANNOT_SAY is what every agent answers until one can declare that it stopped to ask - a run that
  # ended with a question looks exactly like one that finished.
  asked: Asked,
  # Where asked came from: "declared" for a rule the agent declares, "" for none.
  askedFrom: string,
  # The session its agent was running, recorded where the agent declares how to name one - from an
  # unattended run's records, or an attached agent's own session files when the task stops. Starting
  # the task again continues it, and says so. "" when none is recorded: the next start is a fresh
  # session. An identifier, not a credential; gone when the task is removed.
  session: string,
  # What this task was given beyond its agent's own credential, from the project and its run: a vault
  # entry's name to the destination it is for. Names only, never a token.
  credentials: [string]string,
  # For each of those credentials a person granted: who and when, as it stood when the task started - the
  # person this task acts as. Empty when none is a grant.
  grants: [string]Authorization
)

# What became of a Stop or a Remove.
#
# One enumeration serves both because one enumeration implements both. A client should render
# every value it can receive: a refusal is the interesting outcome, not an error case.
type Outcome (
  # Stop: the name is not one Sokar created. Nothing was touched.
  NOT_A_TASK,
  # Stop: no container and no helpers by that name.
  NOTHING_TO_STOP,
  # Stop: the container is down and the helpers are gone.
  STOPPED,
  # Stop: refused, because the task holds commits that never reached the gate. work says what.
  # Re-ask Remove with force to discard it, or with rescue to push it into the mirror first.
  HOLDS_WORK,
  # Stop: nothing could say whether there was work, so it was not removed.
  NOTHING_KNOWS,
  # Stop: rescue was asked for, but the container is already down and cannot be read.
  RESCUE_NEEDS_IT_RUNNING,
  # Stop: rescue was attempted and failed. detail says why. Nothing was removed.
  RESCUE_FAILED,
  # Remove: the container was removed and its helpers were already gone.
  REMOVED,
  # Remove: refused because the task is running. Stop it, or ask again with force.
  STILL_RUNNING
)

# An agent binary installed on this machine.
type Agent (
  # Name the binary calls itself by, which is what Start takes.
  name: string,
  # Human-readable name.
  label: string,
  # The command it runs inside the container.
  binary: string,
  # The build this agent pins, from its own manifest, or "" when it pins none. NOT what the
  # binary answers if asked its version: nothing here executes it to find out, so two machines
  # showing the same value are running the same declared build.
  version: string,
  # Where the binary was found.
  from: string,
  # Hosts this agent needs, added to a task's egress on top of the project's own.
  # How this agent logs itself in, as arguments to its own binary, and where that is described.
  #
  # Declared by the agent, never guessed here: some agents open a browser and some print a link
  # and a code, and only the second kind works on a machine somebody reaches over ssh. A client
  # runs these in a TERMINAL on that machine - the login writes into the agent's own config
  # directory, and 'sokar vault import <agent>' then takes what it wrote into the vault.
  #
  # canLogIn is false for most agents: one whose credential is an API key has nothing to log in
  # to, and the manifest saying nothing is how it says so.
  canLogIn: bool,
  # What to run ON THAT MACHINE, in a terminal, to log this agent in. Empty when it cannot.
  #
  # Answered rather than composed by a client: a client that spells out 'sokar vault login
  # <agent>' is right until the day that verb moves, and then every client is wrong at once.
  loginCommand: []string,
  # What that command will run inside its throwaway container - the agent's own binary and
  # whatever it declared. Shown so a person can see what they are about to start; running it is
  # loginCommand's business, not a client's.
  loginArguments: []string,
  # Where that login is described, or "". Worth reading BEFORE running one on a remote machine.
  loginDocumentation: string,
  allowedDomains: []string,
  # Hosts this agent declares and is deliberately NOT given. Not the same as absent from
  # allowedDomains: these were asked for and refused on purpose, which is worth showing beside
  # what was granted rather than leaving as a silence.
  refusedDomains: []string,
  # What this agent fetches when its image is built, each with the digest that pins it. Empty is
  # a real state and not a gap: an agent that writes its tool into the image fetches nothing, so
  # it has a pinned version and no digest at all.
  artifacts: []InstallArtifact,
  # The identity this agent's commits carry, from its own manifest. This is what tells a person
  # looking at a commit whether an agent or a person wrote it, and it is what the pre-push guard
  # on an operator's own checkout matches on.
  commitsAs: GitIdentity
)

# How an agent signs its work.
type GitIdentity (
  # Author name on its commits.
  name: string,
  # Author address on its commits.
  email: string
)

# One file an agent fetches while its image is built.
type InstallArtifact (
  # Where it is fetched from.
  url: string,
  # Expected SHA-256, or "" when there is none - in which case 'unverified' says whether that is
  # deliberate.
  sha256: string,
  # Absolute path it is installed at.
  target: string,
  # Whether it is knowingly unverifiable. An artifact with no digest and no reason is a mistake;
  # one with a reason is a decision somebody made and can be shown as such.
  unverified: bool,
  # Why it cannot be verified. "" unless 'unverified'.
  reason: string
)

# An installed agent binary that never runs: either an earlier location holds one of the same
# file name, or another binary claimed the same declared name first.
type ShadowedAgent (
  # The binary that is installed and not in use.
  path: string,
  # The one that runs instead. Locations are searched most specific first - the operator's own
  # directory, then the packaged one - so this is a rule and not an accident of scan order.
  usedInstead: string
)

# One entry in the vault. Never its value: this interface cannot be used to read a secret.
type Credential (
  name: string,
  # What kind of credential it is, or "" when nothing recorded it.
  type: string,
  # Length of the stored value, so an interface can show that something is there.
  characters: int,
  # What goes with the secret and is not secret - a client id, a token URL, scopes - by name.
  # Configuration, shown whole; never a secret.
  settings: [string]string,
  # For an entry a person grants (oauth-device, oauth-code): who granted it and when, or absent while
  # nobody has. The grant itself is never shown.
  grant: ?Authorization
)

# Who granted an authorization, and when. Not a secret: the audit fact that a task acts as this person.
type Authorization (
  # The account that ran the grant.
  grantedBy: string,
  # When, ISO 8601.
  grantedAt: string
)

# A service a credential is for that is not a model provider: where it is, and where its key goes.
type Destination (
  name: string,
  label: string,
  # Where requests go, https.
  upstream: string,
  # The header the key goes in, and the text before it.
  authHeader: string,
  authPrefix: string,
  # The URL parameter the key goes in instead of the header, or "" for the header.
  authQuery: string,
  # The file that declares it.
  file: string,
  # Whether a package installed it. A packaged one cannot be changed or removed here; writing one of
  # the same name puts the user's own in its place.
  packaged: bool,
  # Whether it is the one in force for its name. A packaged one the user's own takes the place of is
  # listed too, with this false.
  inForce: bool
)

# A push waiting at the gate for a decision.
type PendingPush (
  # Ref name under refs/sokar/incoming/, which is what Review, Approve and Reject take.
  name: string,
  commit: string,
  # First line of the commit message.
  subject: string,
  # How long it has been waiting, as words: "4 minutes".
  waiting: string,
  # When it arrived, ISO-8601.
  at: string
)

# One file a pending push changes, as a person should meet it in a review.
#
# Ranked by consequence, not by suspicion: nothing here detects anything. DANGEROUS is a fixed list of
# kinds Sokar ships - a CI definition, a build script, a dependency manifest or lockfile, something that
# runs on checkout or when the repository is opened, a file made executable, a symbolic link - and is
# named however small the change. GENERATED and REFORMATTING are what is almost never the finding; they
# are moved to the end, never hidden, and a dangerous file is never either.
type ReviewFile (
  # Its path in the repository, after the change. A rename is a removal and an addition.
  path: string,
  # A, M, D or T, as git reports it.
  status: string,
  # Lines added and removed; -1 for a binary file.
  added: int,
  removed: int,
  # DANGEROUS, ORDINARY, GENERATED or REFORMATTING - the order to read them in.
  rank: string,
  # Why it has that rank, in words; "" for an ORDINARY file.
  reason: string
)

# A curated group of hosts a project can opt into by name.
type EgressSet (
  # What a project writes in its egress.sets list, and what SetEgress takes.
  name: string,
  # Human-readable name, for a listing.
  label: string,
  # The hosts it grants, in the order the file declares them.
  domains: []string
)

# A host a task may reach, and who granted it.
type EgressHost (
  host: string,
  # "agent <name>", "provider <name>", "set <name>", "project" or "upstream". The first grant
  # wins, so a host in both a set and an agent's list is reported as the agent's.
  origin: string
)

# How far a change to a running task goes. There is no default: "this run only" and "this run
# and the project file" are different intentions, and a client that did not say which it meant
# must not have one picked for it.
type Scope (
  # The running task, and nothing else. The project file is untouched and the next task will
  # ask again - a resumed task loses it too, because its ruleset and resolver are rebuilt from
  # what is on disk.
  RUN,
  # The running task, and the project file, so the next task starts with it.
  RUN_AND_PROJECT
)

# What became of widening a running task.
type WidenOutcome (
  # Applied to the running task. persisted says whether the file was written too.
  WIDENED,
  # NarrowTask: taken back from the running task.
  NARROWED,
  # What it would do, having changed nothing.
  PREVIEWED,
  # Every name was already granted to this run.
  NO_CHANGE,
  # No such task, or it is not up. Nothing can be changed in a container that is gone.
  NOT_RUNNING,
  # The project is offline, so its tasks reach nothing and this is not a way around that.
  REFUSED_BY_CLASS,
  # A name asked for is refused for this task - by its agent, its project or its repository - and
  # a widening does not take a refusal back. Nothing was changed; detail names it and who refused.
  REFUSED_NAME,
  # The run WAS widened and the project file was not, because nothing knows where it is. Not a
  # failure: the task can now reach the host, and detail says why the file was not written.
  NO_PROJECT_FILE,
  # The run could not be widened at all. detail says why.
  FAILED
)

# What became of a change to a project's egress.
type EgressOutcome (
  # Applied and written to the project file.
  CHANGED,
  # What it would do. Nothing was written, because dryRun was set.
  PREVIEWED,
  # The file already said that. Nothing was written.
  NO_CHANGE,
  # A named set is not installed on this machine, so the file would name nothing real. Nothing
  # was written; detail says which.
  NO_SUCH_SET,
  # The project's security class forbids declaring egress at all - an offline project declares
  # nothing. detail says so in the words the project reader uses.
  REFUSED_BY_CLASS,
  # The project file could not be read. detail says why.
  UNREADABLE,
  # The change was right and the file could not be written. detail says why; opens and closes
  # still describe what it would have done.
  NOT_WRITTEN
)

# A project this machine knows about.
#
# Not something Sokar creates: a project is somebody's directory with a project.yml in it, and
# this reports what has been seen of one rather than owning it.
type Project (
  # Project name, as its file declares it.
  name: string,
  # offline, guarded or online. "" when no task has recorded one.
  securityClass: string,
  # Absolute path of its project.yml on the daemon's machine, which every gate method and Start
  # take. "" when nothing recorded one, or when the recorded file is no longer there - reported
  # absent rather than as a path nothing can read, so a call made with it cannot fail for a
  # reason that looks like a fault in the daemon.
  file: string,
  # The gate's mirror for it. "" when it has never used the gate.
  mirror: string,
  # How many pushes are waiting for review in that mirror.
  pending: int,
  # How many of its tasks exist right now, running or stopped.
  tasks: int,
  # How many of those are up. A project with none is the ordinary case - between tasks, or after
  # one was stopped and can still be resumed - so both numbers are given rather than one being
  # inferred from the other. This list is every project, not only the busy ones.
  running: int,
  # Whether a task image for this project exists, so work can start here without building one
  # first. That is the whole claim: it does NOT say the image still matches the project file as
  # it stands now. A project that has never run answers false, which is a normal state and not a
  # fault - it means the first task here will spend minutes building before anything happens.
  prepared: bool,
  # Where this account has got following this project's repository, or an empty object when it
  # follows nothing here - which is the ordinary case.
  #
  # Here as well as on Following() because they answer different questions: "what does this account
  # follow" and "is what I am showing in force". Both are rendered from the same values, so they
  # cannot drift.
  following: ?Followed,
  # The repositories this project has, its own first.
  #
  # Never empty for a project whose file can be read: every project has its own repository. Empty
  # means the file could not be read, which is NOT the same as a project with one repository - do
  # not render it as one.
  #
  # Objects rather than parallel lists of names, mirrors and counts: lists indexed together drift
  # the moment one is built from a different source, and the drift is silent.
  repositories: []Repository,
  # ABSENT, READY, STALE or UNKNOWN - what 'prepared' cannot say.
  #
  # STALE means an image exists and was built before the project file changed under it, so work
  # would start in something the file no longer describes and nothing would mention it. That is
  # the state this field exists for; a bool could only ever say whether something was there.
  #
  # UNKNOWN is an image that does not record what it was built from - built by an older Sokar, or
  # belonging to a project whose file has moved. Not stale: nothing here knows either way, and
  # reporting it as stale would send somebody rebuilding for no reason.
  #
  # Only the base image and the image snippet decide this. Egress, limits and the upstream change
  # what a task may do rather than what it is built from, and counting them would call an image
  # stale after an edit that could not have changed it - which teaches people to ignore the word.
  preparedState: string,
  # Commits the upstream has that this project's mirror does not. MEANINGLESS unless
  # behindReason is MEASURED - it is 0 for every other reason, and 0 there does not mean up to
  # date. Never render this number without behindMeasured beside it.
  behind: int,
  # When 'behind' was measured, RFC 3339. "" when it never was.
  #
  # Not optional decoration. The daemon measures on a timer, because a reply field that fetched
  # would make this list occasionally take thirty seconds for reasons nothing on screen explains
  # - so what you get is a number with an age, and the only thing worse than a stale answer is a
  # stale answer that looks fresh.
  behindMeasured: string,
  # Why 'behind' is what it is. New values may appear; render an unrecognised one rather than
  # throwing, by the same rule as every other enumeration here.
  #
  #   MEASURED       the number is real, and behindMeasured says when it was taken
  #   NEVER_CHECKED  nothing has looked yet. NOT the same as being up to date
  #   NO_UPSTREAM    the mirror was created empty, so there is nothing to be behind
  #   OFFLINE        the project reaches nothing by class, so asking would break that promise
  #   FAILED         looking failed; behindDetail says what git said
  behindReason: string,
  # What went wrong, for FAILED. "" otherwise. Prose for a person - never parsed, and never
  # rendered as a number.
  behindDetail: string,
  # What project.yml names under 'credentials:': a vault entry's name to the destination it is for, which
  # every task of the project holds. Names only, never a value.
  credentials: [string]string,
  # The project's conversation - its room - when a peer is reached through a transport's own ('<scheme>:');
  # absent otherwise.
  messages: ?ProjectMessages
)

# A project's conversation, on a transport that keeps one.
type ProjectMessages (
  # The transport it is on, the scheme a peer's address names.
  transport: string,
  # What the transport calls it, "" before it was set up on this machine.
  conversation: string,
  # What the project's messages reach through it, as the transport says.
  reaches: []string,
  # Whether this machine can carry the project's messages now: the transport installed and the
  # conversation set up. With detail when not.
  ready: bool,
  detail: string,
  # True for an offline project: its conversation must be on this machine's loopback.
  loopbackOnly: bool
)

# Someone who has joined a project's conversation.
type MessageMember (
  # The name join was given.
  person: string,
  # The account the transport made for them.
  user: string
)

# One log a task has written.
type Log (
  # Pass this to Tail unchanged.
  name: string,
  # How large it is now.
  bytes: int,
  # When it was last written, ISO-8601.
  at: string,
  # What this log holds, for a name that does not say - absent where it does.
  #
  # Two files need it and they are the two somebody looks for: 'events.jsonl' is what the firewall
  # stopped, and 'reader.err' is the process that records those complaining. A table of names at
  # the client end would drift the day a file is added - the list grows, the new one has no
  # sentence, and it looks like the ordinary case. Only this side knows which files exist.
  #
  # Absent, empty and whitespace all mean the same thing: nothing to say. Sent absent rather than
  # empty, because an empty line under a name reads as a description that failed.
  what: ?string
)

# A connection a task tried to make and was stopped for.
#
# This is the one event with a deadline. The task is blocked while it waits, and the watcher
# gives up after its own timeout - so an interface that shows these must show them as they
# arrive, which is why Prompts is a stream and not a poll.
#
# The answer comes back on the same stream: the same destination again, with verdict set. An
# interface should match it to the question by task and key rather than showing it twice.
type Prompt (
  # Container name, for routing the answer back to the right task.
  task: string,
  # Pass this to Decide unchanged. It is derived from the other fields, but the derivation is
  # the daemon's and a client that rebuilt it would answer a prompt that does not exist.
  key: string,
  # Host or address the task asked for.
  destination: string,
  # tcp or udp.
  protocol: string,
  port: int,
  # When it was blocked, ISO-8601 - and on an event carrying a verdict, when it was decided.
  # The two are the same field because they are the same event twice; keep the question's own
  # value if you want the block time of an answered prompt.
  at: string,
  # Where in the ruleset it was stopped, for an operator who needs to know which rule fired.
  # Empty on an event carrying a verdict: nothing decided it a second time.
  prefix: string,
  # When the watcher gives up, ISO-8601, on an open question. "" when it never will, and "" on
  # an event carrying a verdict - nothing is waiting any more.
  #
  # A moment rather than a length, and computed where the timeout lives: an interface showing
  # "three minutes left" would otherwise be doing arithmetic on a number nobody sent it. It is
  # measured from when the question was asked rather than from "at", because "at" is when the
  # connection was blocked and a reader that has fallen behind would publish a deadline already
  # in the past.
  #
  # It belongs to the question, not to the packet. A dropped connection is retried, so the same
  # destination arrives again while one question is open - "at" moves with each, the deadline does
  # not. Recomputing it per event would hand a countdown a fresh minute every retry and it would
  # never visibly run out.
  deadline: string,
  # A destination is announced as open at most once. Its connection is retried and the watcher
  # sees every retry, but a key it has already answered is not published again - so a client that
  # took a settled question off screen never has it put back with buttons under it.
  #
  # Absent while the question is open. Once it is settled the same destination arrives again
  # carrying "allow", "deny" or "timeout", and that is the only way an interface learns that a
  # prompt ran out: nothing will ask about it again, so a question that simply stops arriving is
  # indistinguishable from one still waiting for its operator.
  verdict: ?string
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- what exists

# Every task on this machine, running or stopped.
method List() -> (tasks: []Task)

# The same answer, again whenever it changes.
#
# Called without "more" this answers once, exactly as List does, so a client that cannot
# stream is not locked out of the data.
#
# Called with "more" it sends the whole list on every change and nothing in between. The age
# in state is deliberately not a change: it moves on its own, and a view that redrew for it
# would redraw every second forever.
method Watch() -> (tasks: []Task)

# Agent binaries installed on this machine.
method Agents() -> (
  # One entry per agent NAME, never two: they are held by name, so a name appears once whatever
  # is installed. A second binary claiming a name already taken does not appear here at all - it
  # lands in 'failures'.
  agents: []Agent,
  # Binaries that are installed and could not be asked what they are, by file name. An agent
  # that fails to describe itself is unusable, and leaving it out would read as not installed.
  # A name collision is NOT here: nothing is broken in that case, one copy runs.
  failures: [string]string,
  # Binaries that are installed and never run. Two causes, one consequence: an earlier location
  # holds the same file name, or another binary claimed the same declared name first. Neither has
  # an Agent entry to carry a flag, which is why this is a list rather than a field on one.
  shadowed: []ShadowedAgent
)

# What the vault holds, without any of it.
method Credentials() -> (
  # Where the vault file is.
  vault: string,
  # Whether that file exists.
  exists: bool,
  credentials: []Credential,
  # Whether the list can be believed. The vault opens only if its passphrase is already in the
  # kernel keyring - a daemon has no terminal to ask at - so an empty list from a locked vault
  # and an empty list from an empty vault are different things an interface must not conflate.
  # False means locked or undecryptable, and only then is there a passphrase to ask for. A vault
  # that does not exist yet is readable and empty: there is nothing to unlock.
  readable: bool,
  # What this machine is CONFIGURED to connect with, which is a different list from the one above.
  # Readable with the vault shut, because it holds no secret - which is what lets an interface say
  # "a credential for this host is configured, unlock the vault" instead of "nothing is here".
  connections: []Connection
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- connecting out

# One credential this machine may use to reach somewhere, described without its secret.
#
# The metadata is NOT secret: what exists, for what, of what kind, under which name. That is why an
# interface may write these records (CredentialDeclare) while no value ever crosses this socket.
type Connection (
  # What the secret is called: a vault entry, a file path, or an environment variable, by source.
  id: string,
  # SSH_KEY, TOKEN, BASIC or OAUTH. Said, never inferred: https may be a token OR a username and
  # password, so the URL cannot decide it.
  kind: string,
  # The destinations this covers, as a prefix of the normalised URL - "ssh://github.com" or
  # "https://gitlab.example/acme/". git@host:path normalises to ssh://host/path. The LONGEST match
  # wins, so one record covers a forge and another covers one group on it.
  match: string,
  # Username, for BASIC and for a token that needs one. "" when the caller's default is fine.
  user: string,
  # What it may be used for: "git", "registry", ... or "any".
  purpose: string,
  # VAULT, FILE, ENVIRONMENT or AGENT.
  #
  # FILE and ENVIRONMENT point at something this machine did not create and does not protect - a
  # key in ~/.ssh, a token already exported - which is what somebody working on their own computer
  # wants, and is a state to SHOW rather than a fault. AGENT reads nothing at all: the command
  # inherits the account's own ssh-agent.
  source: string,
  # Whether the secret is somewhere this machine encrypts and can shut. False for FILE,
  # ENVIRONMENT and AGENT. Show it; do not refuse it.
  protected: bool,
  # Whether the secret is actually there right now: an entry in the vault, a readable file, a set
  # variable. False with a shut vault only tells you the vault is shut - read 'readable' too.
  present: bool,
  # When an OAUTH token stops working, RFC 3339, or "". A machine that knows this refuses first
  # and says what to run, instead of letting the destination answer 401.
  expires: string
)

# Records a credential this machine may use. NO SECRET CROSSES THIS SOCKET.
#
# What this writes is the readable half - kind, destination, username, where the value lives. The
# value itself is typed on the machine, and 'storeCommand' says exactly what to type. For source
# FILE, ENVIRONMENT or AGENT there is nothing to type: the value is already somewhere, and this
# records where.
method CredentialDeclare(
  # What the secret is called. For VAULT the entry name; for FILE the path; for ENVIRONMENT the
  # variable. Omitted for AGENT.
  #
  # Omitted for VAULT too, which means "you name it": the name becomes the one this machine
  # already looks in for that destination - git.ssh.<host> or git.token.<host> - so a credential
  # declared without a name and one stored without a declaration are the same entry. A destination
  # that names no host is refused rather than recorded namelessly.
  id: ?string,
  kind: string,
  match: string,
  user: ?string,
  purpose: ?string,
  # VAULT (the default), FILE, ENVIRONMENT or AGENT.
  source: ?string,
  # A file ON THAT MACHINE the value is in. Only with source VAULT, and only a convenience: the
  # answer's storeCommand then reads that file instead of asking for the value to be piped or
  # typed, so "use the key that is already here" is one command a client runs unchanged. Nothing
  # is read here - the command does it, when somebody runs it.
  fromFile: ?string,
  # Says what declaring this would do, and writes NOTHING. Every refusal a real declaration gives:
  # a kind that cannot open that destination, a public key, a value that is not there, a shut
  # vault. For an ssh key it also asks the forge who that key logs in as - see 'identity' - which
  # catches a key belonging to another account or another repository at the moment somebody
  # chooses it, rather than at the first fetch.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # The record as it was written.
  connection: Connection,
  # What to run ON THAT MACHINE to store the value, as ARGUMENTS rather than a string: a client
  # runs it over ssh and should never have to split a command line it did not write. Empty when
  # there is nothing to store - FILE, ENVIRONMENT and AGENT already have their value somewhere.
  storeCommand: []string,
  # What that command needs on its standard input, in words, or "" when it prompts for the value
  # itself. An ssh KEY is a file and cannot be typed at a prompt, so this says "the private key
  # file" and the client arranges for it; a token is asked for, without echo, and this is "".
  storeStdin: string,
  # Whether this replaced a record that was already there for the same destination and purpose.
  # Declaring twice is not an error - a wizard run again must land in the same place - so this is
  # how an interface says "updated" rather than "added".
  replaced: bool,
  # Whether anything was written. False for a dry run.
  recorded: bool,
  # READY or the reason it would not work, for a dry run. "" for a real declaration, which either
  # succeeds or fails with an error.
  #
  # READY, MISSING_VALUE, NO_VAULT, VAULT_LOCKED, NO_CREDENTIAL, EXPIRED or KEY_REFUSED.
  # NO_VAULT is not MISSING_VALUE: there is nowhere to put anything yet, so 'sokar vault init'
  # comes first and a wizard that sent somebody to 'vault put' would fail at its last step.
  # KEY_REFUSED means the host turned this key away - the record would be perfect and the first
  # fetch would fail.
  outcome: string,
  # What the forge said when asked who this key logs in as, for a dry run of an SSH_KEY - either
  # "Hi fuinorg/utils4j!" or what it refused with - and "" when nothing could be asked. A key that
  # reaches a different account, or a single other repository, or nothing at all, looks perfectly
  # good until the first fetch, and only the machine can ask. When it was refused, 'outcome' is
  # KEY_REFUSED: silence about that would read exactly like "nothing to report".
  identity: string,
  # One sentence for a person, for a dry run. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

# Forgets a credential record. The secret is NOT removed: a key in ~/.ssh is the person's, and a
# vault entry is removed with 'sokar vault remove' by somebody at the machine.
method CredentialForget(
  # The record's 'match', exactly as Connection gives it.
  match: string
) -> (
  # Whether a record was there to forget.
  forgotten: bool,
  # What still holds a value, so an interface can offer the second step rather than implying the
  # secret is gone. "" when nothing does.
  leftBehind: string
)

# One ssh key this account has, described without its value.
type SshKey (
  # Where it is. This is what a FILE credential names.
  path: string,
  # The algorithm, or "" when a passphrase hides it - an encrypted key says nothing about itself.
  type: string,
  # SHA256:... of the public half, or "" when it cannot be worked out.
  fingerprint: string,
  # What the public half calls it, usually an address. Often the only thing that tells two keys
  # apart to the person who made them.
  comment: string,
  # Whether a passphrase protects it.
  encrypted: bool,
  # Whether the PRIVATE half is there. False means only a .pub was found - which is not a
  # credential, and is the mistake anybody makes once.
  privateHalf: bool,
  # Whether this machine can sign with it as it stands.
  usable: bool,
  # DIRECTORY (found in ~/.ssh) or CONFIGURED (named by an IdentityFile line in ~/.ssh/config).
  found: string,
  # Why it cannot be used as it stands, with what to do about it, or "".
  obstacle: string
)

# One key a host offers, as this machine saw it.
type HostKey (
  # The algorithm, as ssh names it.
  type: string,
  # SHA256:... - the form ssh itself prints, and the form a person is told out of band.
  fingerprint: string
)

# What a host offers, so a person can compare it with what they were told.
#
# Shown, never recorded: recording is TrustHostKey, which takes the fingerprint back. Accepting a
# host key on first use would be easier and is exactly what an interception looks like, so this
# machine refuses to decide it - attended or not, because a timer that learns a host at three in
# the morning has answered the question this refuses to answer.
method HostKeys(
  host: string
) -> (
  host: string,
  # Whether this machine already remembers a key for it.
  known: bool,
  keys: []HostKey
)

# Records ONE key for a host: the one a person confirmed.
#
# The fingerprint comes from them, not from here. The host is asked again and the key is recorded
# only if it still offers that one - which is what stops a key that arrived between being shown
# and being confirmed from being the one written down.
method TrustHostKey(
  host: string,
  # SHA256:..., exactly as HostKeys gave it.
  fingerprint: string
) -> (
  recorded: bool,
  type: string,
  fingerprint: string,
  detail: string
)

# The ssh keys this account already has.
#
# So that "use the key that is already here" is a choice from a list rather than a path typed from
# memory. NO VALUE is ever read out of here: a key is opened far enough to say what it is and
# whether a passphrase protects it.
#
# Deciding what is a private key means reading files, which is why this is answered by the machine
# rather than by a client listing a home directory over ssh.
method SshKeys() -> (
  keys: []SshKey
)

# Which credential a destination would use, and whether it would work - before anything is tried.
#
# The call a dialog makes before offering 'Follow it' or 'Approve': it answers the same reasons
# Follow(dryRun) does, without touching the network.
method CredentialCheck(
  # Where something would connect, such as a project repository's URL.
  url: string,
  # What for. Default: "git".
  purpose: ?string
) -> (
  # READY, NO_CREDENTIAL, VAULT_LOCKED, MISSING_VALUE, EXPIRED or NOT_NEEDED.
  #
  # NOT_NEEDED is a local path: nothing authenticates, and offering to store something would waste
  # somebody's afternoon. MISSING_VALUE means a record exists and its value does not - the file is
  # gone, the variable is unset, the vault entry was never stored.
  outcome: string,
  # The record that would be used, or an empty one.
  connection: Connection,
  # What to run on that machine to fix it, as arguments. Empty when nothing would help - a local
  # path, or a value this machine does not keep.
  storeCommand: []string,
  # What that command needs on its standard input, or "". See CredentialDeclare.
  storeStdin: string,
  # One sentence for a person.
  detail: string
)

# The purposes a credential may serve: "git" and "any". "git" is the only one anything reads
# today; a record with another is kept and used by nothing. When a second consumer appears it will
# be named here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- running tasks

# Starts a task: creates one that is not there, or brings back one that is stopped.
#
# One verb, because the caller cannot know which case they are in - and pressing is not how
# somebody should find out. That is what startAction on the listing is for. A task that is already
# running is refused rather than started twice.
#
# With "more" it streams the build as it happens, one reply per line, and an interface should show
# them: building an image takes minutes, and showing nothing for that long is indistinguishable
# from having hung. With "now" it returns as soon as the task exists and the build goes on without
# it; the task is in List and Watch immediately with its phase set, and Tail reads the output.
#
# The task outlives this call and this connection either way. Closing the socket mid-build does not
# cancel it; List shows it afterwards.
method Start(
  # Task name within the project. Default: "shell".
  task: ?string,
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it. REQUIRED - there is no default directory any more.
  project: ?string,
  # Agent to run, by the name Agents reports. Default: the only one installed, or an error naming
  # them all when several are.
  agent: ?string,
  # Provider to route through. Default: the agent's own declared default, or an error when it
  # declares none. A project file names neither an agent nor a provider.
  provider: ?string,
  # Overrides the stored credential kind for this run.
  credentialType: ?string,
  # Lifetime of the gate token. Default: 8.
  tokenHours: ?int,
  # Upstream to gate against. Default: the project's own.
  upstream: ?string,
  # Which of the project's repositories this task works on. REQUIRED, and there is no default.
  #
  # A project is a unit of work over one or more repositories, and a task works on exactly one of
  # them. The project's own repository - where project.yml, the planning and the issues live - is
  # one of the choices, and is how an agent gets a task for planning. Project.repositories lists
  # what to offer, its own first.
  #
  # Not defaulted even when a project has exactly one. A default would be paid for later and
  # somewhere else: a project that grew a second repository would silently change what an existing
  # call does. Omitting it is refused, and the refusal names what there is to choose from.
  repository: ?string,
  # Credentials this task holds beyond the agent's own and the project's: a vault entry's name to the
  # destination or provider it is for. They add to the project's and cannot point one of its elsewhere.
  # Each reaches the task as SOKAR_TOKEN_<ENTRY> and SOKAR_URL_<ENTRY>, a token worthless anywhere else.
  credentials: ?[string]string,
  # Runs with no workspace and no git gate: the agent gets an empty directory and can commit
  # nowhere.
  noGate: ?bool,
  # Does everything up to starting the container, then stops and reports. Nothing is created.
  dryRun: ?bool,
  # What to do with a blocked connection: prompt, allow, deny or off. Default: "prompt".
  clearance: ?string,
  # Removes the container when the session ends. Default: false.
  #
  # REPLACES 'keep', whose default was the other way round: the command a person reaches for first
  # removed what it had just made, which is how an operator lost a task by typing the obvious
  # thing. Keeping is the safe default; throwing away is the deliberate one.
  rm: ?bool,
  # Returns as soon as the task exists, instead of waiting for the build to finish.
  #
  # Named for what it does rather than 'detach': over this interface Start never attaches anything,
  # so there is nothing to detach from. The command line has a second switch for not attaching a
  # terminal, which is a question only a terminal has.
  now: ?bool,
  # How somebody is meant to be involved, recorded on the task and answered by List and Watch.
  # Default: UNATTENDED when a prompt is given, SHELL otherwise - so a caller that gives a prompt
  # need not repeat itself, and one that gives neither gets the honest answer for a container
  # nobody is attached to.
  mode: ?Mode,
  # What an unattended task is asked to do. Recorded on the task and kept after it finishes.
  #
  # Giving one makes this call RUN the agent, not merely start the container: the call lasts as
  # long as the run does, and with "more" the agent's own output arrives as it is produced. The
  # exit code in the reply is then the agent's. Without a prompt the container comes up and the
  # call returns, as it always did.
  prompt: ?string,
  # Model to ask the agent for, when it declares a model flag. Ignored otherwise.
  model: ?string,
  # Turn limit, when the agent declares one.
  maxTurns: ?int,
  # How long the agent may run, in minutes. Default: 30. On expiry the run is killed and whatever
  # it produced is kept - the exit code is 124 and the log holds what it managed to say.
  minutes: ?int
) -> (
  # One line of output. Set on every reply while continues is true, and only then.
  line: ?string,
  # Container name of the started task. Set on the final reply, and only then.
  container: ?string,
  # What the launch returned. 0 is success. Set on the final reply.
  exitCode: ?int,
  # Everything that was printed, for a caller that did not ask to stream. Empty when it did.
  output: ?[]string,
  # What it did, or the refusal it answered with. Set on the final reply: CREATE or RESUME for what
  # it did; RUNNING, NEEDS_VAULT or PREDATES_RESTART for a refusal.
  #
  # A refusal is an ordinary reply, not an error, and carries container and exitCode like any
  # other - the exit code is the refusal's. NEEDS_VAULT in particular arrives this way, so an
  # interface can offer to unlock the vault rather than report a failure.
  action: ?StartAction,
  # How many recorded helpers were started, and how many the task is recorded as having had. Fewer
  # started than recorded is a partial start and an interface should say so. Absent when the task
  # was created rather than resumed - 0 of 0 would claim an answer to a question nobody asked.
  helpersStarted: ?int,
  helpersRecorded: ?int,
  # What went wrong per helper, for the ones that did not come back.
  problems: ?[]string,
  # Set when the image the container was built from has changed since it was created.
  imageDrift: ?string
)

# Stops a task and the helpers Sokar started beside it.
#
# The container is stopped, NOT removed: its filesystem is the task's workspace, so removing it
# would throw away the agent's work. Removal is Remove's decision, and asking for it here is what
# purge used to do.
#
# On the way down it writes what the task holds, while that is still knowable - whoever removes it
# later cannot ask the container itself.
method Stop(
  # Container name.
  task: string
) -> (
  outcome: Outcome,
  # How many helper processes were stopped.
  helpers: int,
  # How many helper processes are still alive afterwards. Not zero is worth showing.
  surviving: int,
  # Why, in words, for an outcome that needs one. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

# Removes a task: its container, its state, and the workspace inside it.
#
# Refuses rather than destroys, in three ways, and an interface should render each as a question
# rather than an error:
#
#   STILL_RUNNING   it is up. Stop it first, or pass force.
#   HOLDS_WORK      it holds commits that never reached the gate. 'work' says what. Ask again with
#                   rescue to push them into the mirror first, or force to discard them.
#   NOTHING_KNOWS   nothing could say what it holds, so nothing was removed.
#
# The record NOTHING_KNOWS rests on is written by Stop. Where that record lives decides whether
# this guard survives a restart of the machine; see the note on the runtime directory.
method Remove(
  # Container name.
  task: string,
  # Push what it holds into the gate's mirror before removing it.
  rescue: ?bool,
  # Remove it anyway: stops it if it is running, and discards work that never reached the gate.
  # One flag for both refusals, deliberately - it is the "I mean it" answer, and splitting it would
  # invite passing whichever one happens to unblock this run without reading the other.
  force: ?bool
) -> (
  outcome: Outcome,
  # What it held, when that is why it was refused. "" otherwise.
  work: string,
  # Where rescued work was put. "" when none was.
  rescuedRef: string,
  # Whether the container was removed.
  removed: bool,
  # Why, in words, for an outcome that needs one. "" otherwise.
  detail: string,
  # How many paths the container had that its image did not - what the agent installed inside it,
  # which is destroyed with the container and has nowhere to arrive, unlike the workspace. Counted
  # only when it was removed, and zero otherwise. Show it beside the removal: nothing else records
  # that any of it existed.
  discarded: int
)

# One task a panic stopped, or would have stopped.
type PanickedTask (
  # Container name, which is what Start takes to bring it back.
  name: string,
  # How many helper processes it had.
  helpers: int,
  # Helpers that outlived the stop and have to be killed by hand. Empty is the normal case.
  surviving: []string
)

# Stops every running task and every helper any of them started.
#
# No task names, on purpose: somebody reaching for this is not in a position to list what is
# running first, and a stop that needs an inventory is one they will get wrong.
#
# IT STOPS; IT NEVER REMOVES. Every workspace, log and unpushed commit survives, and Start brings
# a task back with the work it had. An interface that presented this as a cleanup would send
# somebody looking for work that is still exactly where it was. Discarding is a separate decision,
# made afterwards, by name, with Remove.
method Panic(
  # Lists what would be stopped and stops nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # Every task that was running, in the order they were stopped. Empty means nothing was running.
  tasks: []PanickedTask,
  # Every helper that outlived its stop, across all tasks. Non-empty means somebody has processes
  # to kill by hand, and they are named rather than counted.
  surviving: []string,
  # Whether this only listed what would happen.
  previewed: bool
)

# Drops the cached vault passphrase, so the next thing that needs it has to ask.
#
# The only call that changes the credential store. There is deliberately no Unlock: a daemon has
# no terminal to take a passphrase at, so it can shut the vault and can never open it - unlocking
# stays at the machine, with 'sokar vault unlock'.
method Lock() -> (
  # Whether the kernel keyring exists here at all. Without it nothing can be cached, so nothing
  # ever was, and "locked" would claim a change that did not happen.
  keyring: bool,
  # Whether a passphrase was cached and has now been dropped. False means it was already locked -
  # which is the wanted state either way, so this is not a failure.
  wasCached: bool,
  # Running tasks whose credential proxy already read the credential and holds it in its own
  # memory, where locking cannot reach. Stopping them is what ends that. An interface reporting
  # "locked" without this would be claiming more than happened.
  holding: int
)

# Why work could not start here, or that it could.
#
# New values may appear; render an unrecognised one rather than throwing, by the same rule as
# every other enumeration here. The three that mean different ACTIONS are NO_PROVIDER_CHOSEN
# (pick a provider), CREDENTIAL_MISSING (store a secret) and VAULT_LOCKED (unlock it) - showing
# any of those as another is telling somebody to do the wrong thing.
type StartOutcome (
  # Nothing is in the way.
  READY,
  # The project file is not there, or cannot be read.
  NO_PROJECT_FILE,
  # The task name is one Start would refuse: lowercase letters, digits and inner hyphens, not
  # only digits, and 'sokar-<project>-<task>' at most 65 characters. 'detail' says which.
  BAD_TASK_NAME,
  # No repository was named and Start never picks one, not even for a project that has exactly
  # one. Somebody chooses a REPOSITORY; Project.repositories lists them, its own first, and
  # 'detail' names them too. This is the outcome a start dialog turns into its required choice.
  NO_REPOSITORY_CHOSEN,
  # A repository was named and the project has none of that name. 'detail' lists what it has.
  UNKNOWN_REPOSITORY,
  # Nothing is installed to run. A task with no agent can still be started as a plain shell, so
  # this is not "the machine is broken".
  NO_AGENT,
  # A name was given and nothing is installed under it.
  UNKNOWN_AGENT,
  # Several are installed and none was named. Somebody chooses an agent.
  SEVERAL_AGENTS,
  # The agent names no default provider and none was chosen. Somebody chooses a PROVIDER - not a
  # secret, which is why this is not folded into CREDENTIAL_MISSING.
  NO_PROVIDER_CHOSEN,
  # The provider asked for is not declared on this machine.
  UNKNOWN_PROVIDER,
  # The provider is declared but does not serve the dialect this agent speaks.
  WRONG_DIALECT,
  # The vault cannot be read, so this question cannot be answered yet. NOT a missing credential:
  # the one thing that helps is unlocking, and telling somebody to store a secret they already
  # have is the instruction that cannot help them.
  VAULT_LOCKED,
  # The vault is readable and holds nothing under the name this run would look for. 'credential'
  # names it.
  CREDENTIAL_MISSING,
  # Something is stored and cannot be brokered - the wrong kind for this provider. 'detail' says
  # what.
  CREDENTIAL_UNUSABLE,
  # A credential the project or the run names is for a destination nobody declared here, or the run points
  # one of the project's elsewhere. 'credential' names it; 'detail' says whether the project or the run
  # named it. Start refuses the same case with NoSuchDestination, before anything is launched.
  UNKNOWN_DESTINATION,
  # A credential the project or the run names is one a person grants (oauth-device, oauth-code), and
  # nobody has granted it. 'credential' names the entry; 'detail' gives the command that grants it, and
  # Authorize is how an interface grants it. Start refuses an unattended or agent run for it; a shell
  # starts with a warning. A grant that ended at the service is not seen here: it is found when spent.
  AUTHORIZATION_NEEDED
)

# Whether work could start here, asked before anything is created.
#
# This exists because the only other way to find out was to start a task and read an exit code and
# a stream of prose. It takes the same choices Start takes and answers from the same code, so a
# check that says READY and a Start that refuses cannot disagree.
#
# What it CANNOT tell you: whether the credential is still valid at the provider. It answers that
# something usable is stored under the name this run would look for, which is a different claim.
method CanStart(
  # Project NAME, as Start takes it. Omitted, no project is checked - which is how an interface
  # asks about the machine before a project has been chosen. A name this machine does not have is
  # an ANSWER here (NO_PROJECT_FILE, naming what it does have), not an error.
  project: ?string,
  # Which of the project's repositories the task would be for, exactly as Start takes it.
  # Omitted with a project given, the answer is NO_REPOSITORY_CHOSEN - because that is what Start
  # would do. Pass noGate when the run would have no gate: such a run works on no repository.
  repository: ?string,
  # Whether the run would have no workspace and no git gate, as Start takes it.
  noGate: ?bool,
  # The task name Start would be given. Omitted, no name is checked. The length rule needs the
  # project, so without one only the characters are.
  task: ?string,
  # Agent to run. Omitted, the only one installed.
  agent: ?string,
  # Provider to route through. Omitted, the agent's own declared default.
  provider: ?string,
  # Overrides the stored credential kind for this question, as Start takes it.
  credentialType: ?string,
  # The credentials the run would add, as Start takes them. Each the project or the run names must be for a
  # destination declared here, or the answer is UNKNOWN_DESTINATION.
  credentials: ?[string]string
) -> (
  # Whether work could start. True exactly when outcome is READY, so branch on whichever suits -
  # but never infer the ACTION from this alone: three different things somebody has to do all
  # arrive as false.
  ready: bool,
  outcome: StartOutcome,
  # The agent this was answered for. "" when none was settled.
  agent: string,
  # The provider serving it. "" when the agent is not brokered, or none was settled.
  provider: string,
  # The vault key this run would look for. "" when the question never got that far.
  #
  # The name ACTUALLY looked for, not the one that ought to apply: a vault written before
  # credentials were keyed by provider still answers under the agent's own name, so this can be
  # either. Render this, not a name of your own composing - otherwise the sentence on screen names
  # a key that would be right in a vault the person does not have.
  credential: string,
  # What to run ON THAT MACHINE to store it, as arguments, with CREDENTIAL_MISSING. Empty
  # otherwise. Answered here rather than joined together from Providers() by a client: it is a
  # string this already builds, and a second place that builds it is a second place it can drift.
  storeCommand: []string,
  # Prose for a person, always set. Never parsed.
  detail: string
)

# Gives a task a changeable caption, or removes one.
#
# NOT a rename. A task's name is its identity in four places - the container, the gate ref it
# pushes to, the workspace and the log files - and moving a ref that may have unreviewed pushes
# behind it is a different and much larger thing. This changes what a person reads in a list and
# nothing else.
method Label(
  # Task name, as List reports it.
  task: string,
  # The caption. Omitted or blank removes it.
  label: ?string
) -> (
  #   LABELLED      the caption was written down
  #   CLEARED       the caption was removed
  #   NOT_A_TASK    no such task, or its state directory is gone
  #   NOT_RECORDED  it exists but recorded nothing about itself - started by an older Sokar
  #   FAILED        the profile could not be written
  outcome: string,
  # What the task's caption is now. "" after CLEARED, and "" for every outcome that changed
  # nothing.
  label: string
)

# Reads one of a task's log files, and with "more" follows it as it is written.
#
# The log name is checked against the files that are there rather than resolved as a path.
# The curated egress sets installed on this machine.
#
# What a chooser offers, so nobody has to author a host list. Scanned from a directory each time
# it is asked, so a set an operator dropped in appears without anything being rebuilt - and
# theirs wins over a packaged one of the same name, which is why the locations are given in the
# order they are searched.
#
# Not every set can be complete: os-packages-fedora reaches mirrors named by a mirrorlist, which
# differ by region and by day, so hosts beyond the list arrive as clearance prompts. An interface
# should not present it as equivalent to the others.
method Sets() -> (
  sets: []EgressSet,
  # Directories searched, most specific first. Empty means none is installed.
  locations: []string
)

# What a project may reach, and who granted each host.
#
# The agent's own hosts and its provider's are included, because what an operator wants to know
# is what a task will reach and half of it is not in the project file. Which agent that is
# follows the same rule a run does: the one named, or the only one installed.
method Egress(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Which of its repositories to answer for. Default: the project's own.
  #
  # A repository's grants are ADDED to the project's, so without this you get the project-level
  # set - true of every repository - and with it that set PLUS what the repository adds. Each host
  # says which of the two granted it, in EgressHost.origin: "project", "set <name>", or
  # "repository <name>".
  repository: ?string,
  # Agent whose grants to include. Default: the only one installed.
  agent: ?string
) -> (
  hosts: []EgressHost,
  # Hosts an agent declares it asks for and is deliberately not given. Distinguishable from a
  # host nobody added, which is the distinction the firewall itself cannot make.
  refused: []string
)

# Changes what a project may reach, or says what a change would do.
#
# The highest-consequence edit in the product, so it answers in hosts rather than in set names: a
# set is a name for several hosts and whoever adds one is entitled to see them. The project file
# is edited in place - comments, key order and quoting survive - and the result is parsed by the
# reader that would have to read it later before anything is written.
#
# Nothing here applies to a task that is already running: a container's ruleset and resolver are
# built when it starts.
method SetEgress(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Which of the project's repositories to declare this for. Default: the project's own.
  #
  # A repository's grants are ADDED to the project's, so declaring one here never takes away what
  # the project granted - and never widens the project's other repositories, which is the whole
  # reason this parameter exists. A repository this file does not name is refused rather than
  # written to the project's block.
  repository: ?string,
  # Curated sets to declare. A set this machine does not have is refused rather than written.
  addSets: ?[]string,
  removeSets: ?[]string,
  # Hosts to declare directly, for a mirror no set covers.
  addDomains: ?[]string,
  removeDomains: ?[]string,
  # Stop before writing and report what would have happened.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  outcome: EgressOutcome,
  # Hosts the change adds, with the origin that grants each. In the order they were granted.
  opens: []EgressHost,
  # Hosts it takes away.
  closes: []EgressHost,
  # What reaching a forge costs a guarded project, when this change opens one and the project
  # did not already reach one. "" otherwise - said once, when it becomes true.
  cost: string,
  # Why it was refused, or what could not be written. "" when nothing went wrong.
  detail: string
)

# Gives a task that is already running somewhere new to reach.
#
# The alternative is to stop the run and start another one, which throws away a workspace, a gate
# token, commits that never reached the gate and an agent halfway through something - to add one
# host. This changes it in place: the resolver is told without being restarted, and the name is
# recorded so the clearance watcher lets it through without asking anybody.
#
# Names only. A set is a name for several hosts and granting one would be the same operation
# repeated; narrowing a running task is not offered at all, because taking a grant away from a
# container that already has it is a different thing and is not decided yet.
#
# **The attempt that was already refused is not retried.** The packet was dropped; whether the
# agent tries again is the agent's business. An interface may say the host is now reachable and
# must not say that the request which just failed will now succeed.
# Takes names back from a running task, and optionally from its project file.
#
# THIS STOPS NEW CONNECTIONS AND NOT THE ONES ALREADY RUNNING. The name stops resolving and its
# recorded addresses come out of the firewall, so nothing new can be reached - but the ruleset
# accepts established traffic without consulting the set again, so a transfer in progress runs to
# its end. Do not render this as "the host is now unreachable": it is not, yet. If a transfer has
# to stop, stopping the task is what does that.
#
# The addresses removed are the ones recorded when each grant was applied, never the answer to
# resolving the name again now - a CDN or round-robin answers the daemon and the container
# differently, and the addresses that differ are exactly the ones that would be left open.
#
# 'scope' is required, exactly as for WidenTask. Narrowing only the run when somebody meant the
# project too leaves the next task starting with the host still open.
# Turns enforcement on or off on a task that is already running.
#
# Deliberately not WidenTask with a special value. Those grant and withdraw names, and that the
# firewall stays loaded is what they mean; folding "stop asking about anything" into them would
# make one method mean two unrelated things, and the quiet one would be the dangerous one.
#
# What it cannot undo: turning enforcement off does not recall a connection that was already
# refused - the packet was dropped and nothing retries it - and turning it back on does not recall
# anything waved through while it was off. The ruleset is loaded throughout, so this never opens a
# destination by itself; what changes is whether a blocked connection produces a question.
method SetClearance(
  task: string,
  # prompt, allow, deny or off.
  mode: string,
  # Says what would happen and changes nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # CHANGED, UNCHANGED, PREVIEWED, NO_SUCH_TASK, NOT_RUNNING, UNKNOWN_MODE, NOT_RECORDED or
  # FAILED.
  #
  # UNCHANGED is not a failure: it was already in that mode and nothing was restarted, which is
  # worth saying rather than reporting a change that did not happen.
  outcome: string,
  # The mode before. "" for a task started before this was recorded.
  was: string,
  # The mode after. "" when nothing changed.
  now: string,
  detail: string
)

method NarrowTask(
  # Task name, as List reports it.
  task: string,
  # Names to take back. Anything not currently granted to this run is ignored, not an error.
  domains: []string,
  # RUN or RUN_AND_PROJECT. Required; there is no default.
  scope: Scope,
  # Reports what it would take back, having changed nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  #   NARROWED         taken back from the run; persisted says whether the file was too
  #   PREVIEWED        what it would take back, having changed nothing
  #   NO_CHANGE        none of these names is granted to this run
  #   NOT_RUNNING      no such task, or it is not up
  #   NO_PROJECT_FILE  the RUN was narrowed and the file was not; NOT a failure
  #   FAILED           the run could not be narrowed; detail says why
  outcome: WidenOutcome,
  # Names taken back, in the order they were asked for.
  closes: []string,
  # How many addresses came out of the firewall. Zero with a non-empty 'closes' is a real state:
  # the name was granted and the container never reached it, so nothing was in the set.
  addresses: int,
  # Whether the project file was changed as well.
  persisted: bool,
  # Why it was refused or what went wrong. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

method WidenTask(
  # Container name, from Task.name.
  task: string,
  # Host names to grant. A grant covers what is under it, as the resolver does.
  domains: []string,
  # Required. RUN or RUN_AND_PROJECT.
  scope: Scope,
  # Stop before changing anything and report what would happen.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  outcome: WidenOutcome,
  # The names it grants, in the order they were asked for.
  opens: []string,
  # Whether the project file was written as well. Always false for RUN.
  persisted: bool,
  # Why it was refused, or what could not be written. "" when nothing went wrong.
  detail: string
)

# Every project this machine knows about, alphabetically.
#
# The list is assembled from what is durable: the gate mirrors, the tasks that exist, and the
# project files task starts have recorded. A project appears here once somebody has run a task
# with it - there is no registration step, because nobody would run one.
#
# This is the method that makes the gate reachable from an interface. Pending, Review, Approve,
# Reject and Start all take a project file path, and a client on another machine has no
# filesystem here to find one in.
# One thing a project deletion removes.
type Removal (
  # MIRROR, IMAGE, BUILD, REGISTRY, UPSTREAM_RECORD or TASK.
  kind: string,
  # The thing itself - a path, an image name, a container name - as a person would recognise it.
  what: string
)

type DeleteOutcome (
  # Everything Sokar built for it is gone.
  DELETED,
  # What would go, having removed nothing.
  PREVIEWED,
  # Refused: work reached the gate and nobody reviewed it. The same name Stop uses, for the same
  # situation, so it can read the same on a screen.
  HOLDS_WORK,
  # Refused: tasks are still up.
  TASKS_RUNNING,
  # Nothing here knows that project.
  NO_SUCH_PROJECT,
  # Something could not be removed. detail says what.
  FAILED
)

# DeleteProject is gone; Unfollow is the whole of it.
#
# A project exists on a machine because that machine follows its repository, so removing what Sokar
# built while going on following it would only mean building it again at the next reconciliation.
# Unfollow forgets the repository AND removes the mirrors, the image, the build directory, the
# registry entry, the recorded distance, the followed clone and every task - with the same refusal
# when work is held or a task is up, and the same --force past it.


method Projects() -> (projects: []Project)

# The same answer, again whenever it changes.
#
# Called without "more" this answers once, exactly as Projects does, so a client that cannot
# stream is not locked out of the data.
#
# Called with "more" it sends the whole list on every change and nothing in between. Unlike
# Watch there is no age to exclude: every field here is a count, a state or a recorded
# moment, and "behindMeasured" moving IS a change - the age an interface draws beside the
# number resets with it.
#
# Slower than Watch on purpose. A scan runs the container runtime and reads the gate's refs
# for every project, and what it answers changes on human timescales: a project created, a
# push arriving at the gate, an environment prepared. The one place where latency costs
# something real is Prompts, where a question expires; work waiting at the gate has no
# deadline.
method WatchProjects() -> (projects: []Project)

# CreateProject is gone.
#
# A project comes to be on a machine by that machine following its repository, and nothing else.
# Sokar writes no project.yml anywhere: a person writes one in their repository and commits it, and
# what is wrong with it comes back from Follow as a named reason. A call that rendered and checked
# one beforehand would be a second place the same question could be answered differently - and a
# second way to have a project the machine never verified.
#
# There is no replacement and no render-only form. Offer "follow this repository" instead.


# Something wrong with one answer.
type Problem (
  # Which answer, named as the call named it.
  field: string,
  what: string,
  # Whether it stops the project being created. A non-fatal one is worth showing and not worth
  # blocking on - a base image that is not on the machine yet will simply be pulled.
  fatal: bool
)

# Builds a project's task image without starting anything.
#
# The first task in a project otherwise waits minutes for something that has nothing to do with
# the work. Use with "more": a build takes minutes, and showing nothing for that long is
# indistinguishable from having hung.
method Prepare(
  # Absolute path of the project file, as Projects reports it.
  project: string,
  # Whose tooling to install. Omit when only one agent is installed. An image with no agent is a
  # real thing to want - it is what somebody working in the container by hand needs - so this is
  # not refused when no agent is found.
  agent: ?string,
  # CACHED, AGENT or EVERYTHING. Default CACHED.
  #
  # CACHED is not "skip the build": the build runs and podman's cache decides layer by layer, so
  # an edited project file rebuilds what it changed and nothing else. It is what every task start
  # already does.
  #
  # AGENT replaces the agent's tooling and keeps the base image and its packages. There is no
  # podman flag for "rebuild from here" - it works by changing a build argument placed where the
  # agent's layers begin, which invalidates the cache from that line down. So it is real, and it
  # is a mechanism rather than a switch.
  #
  # EVERYTHING discards the base image's packages too, which means downloading them again.
  rebuild: ?string,
  # Says what it would do and does nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # PREPARED, PREVIEWED, NO_SUCH_PROJECT or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # The image name, or "" when nothing was built.
  image: string,
  # The depth actually used, so an unrecognised one is visible rather than silently defaulted.
  rebuild: string,
  # Progress, when this was not called with "more". Empty when it was streamed.
  output: []string,
  # Why it failed, naming the step. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

# Measures one repository's distance from its upstream, now.
#
# Its own operation on purpose. The distance is otherwise measured on a timer and read from a
# record, because measuring reaches the network - and a listing that fetched would make the queue
# cost what a listing must not. So this cannot be a flag on a read; it is something somebody asked
# for.
#
# It goes through the same measurement the timer uses and writes the same record, so a triggered
# fetch and a timed one cannot disagree about how far behind a project is, and Projects shows
# whichever ran last without being told.
method SyncUpstream(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Which of the project's repositories to measure. Default: the project's own.
  #
  # Each has its own mirror and its own upstream, so a project's distance was never one number
  # once it had a second repository. Measuring one and showing it against all of them would be a
  # number that is right about one repository.
  repository: ?string) -> (
  # MEASURED, NO_SUCH_PROJECT, NO_MIRROR, UNREADABLE or FAILED.
  #
  # NO_SUCH_PROJECT also answers a repository the project does not have; 'detail' says which.
  #
  # NO_MIRROR is ordinary: a project that has never used the gate has nothing to measure against.
  outcome: string,
  # Commits the upstream has that this repository's mirror does not. MEANINGLESS unless
  # 'measured'.
  behind: int,
  # Whether the number above means anything. False for a repository with no upstream, and for one
  # this machine could not reach.
  measured: bool,
  # What the measurement itself reported: MEASURED, NEVER_CHECKED, NO_UPSTREAM, OFFLINE or FAILED.
  reason: string,
  detail: string
)

# What backups have been taken of a project's mirror.
#
# A bundle is written wherever an operator names it, so nothing could work this out afterwards -
# this is read from a record written when each one was taken. An interface cannot offer to restore
# from a list it cannot read, which is what this is for.
#
# Newest first. Empty is an ordinary answer: a project nobody has backed up, or one backed up by a
# Sokar older than the record.
method Backups(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Which of the project's repositories the backups are of. Default: the project's own.
  #
  # A bundle belongs to ONE repository's mirror. Asking without this and restoring what comes back
  # into a different repository would write one history over another's.
  repository: ?string) -> (backups: []Backup)

type Backup (
  # When it was written, ISO-8601.
  taken: string,
  # Absolute path on the daemon's machine.
  bundle: string,
  # How many pushes were waiting for review when it was taken. What a restore would bring back.
  refs: int,
  # Whether that file is still there. A record is not the bundle: the file can be moved, deleted
  # or replaced afterwards and nothing here would know, so an absent one is shown rather than
  # quietly dropped - somebody moved it, and that is worth seeing.
  present: bool,
  # Its size now, or 0 when it is gone. Not what it was when taken: this is read from disk, and
  # claiming a size for a file that is not there would be inventing one.
  bytes: int
)

# Restores a project's mirror from a bundle.
#
# Refuses when the mirror holds pushes nobody has reviewed, and names them. Those exist ONLY in the
# mirror - not on the upstream, not in a workspace, not in the bundle being restored - so
# overwriting one destroys the only copy. Same outcome name as DeleteProject and Stop, for the same
# situation.
method RestoreBackup(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Which of the project's repositories to restore INTO. Default: the project's own.
  #
  # This writes over a mirror, and the pushes it destroys exist only there - so a bundle taken of
  # one repository restored into another destroys the only copy of work that was never its. Pass
  # the repository the bundle came from, as Backups was asked for it.
  repository: ?string,
  # Absolute path of the bundle, as Backups reports it.
  bundle: string,
  # Says what would happen and changes nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool,
  # Proceeds although unreviewed work would be destroyed.
  force: ?bool
) -> (
  # RESTORED, PREVIEWED, HOLDS_WORK, NO_SUCH_PROJECT, NO_SUCH_BACKUP or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # The mirror that would be written, or was.
  mirror: string,
  # Refs nobody reviewed. Non-empty with HOLDS_WORK, and also under force - it is what force
  # destroys, so it is worth showing afterwards as well as before.
  unreviewed: []string,
  detail: string
)

# Removes one backup: the bundle file and the record of it.
#
# The bundle may be somebody's only copy of work that never reached the gate, so this takes the
# path rather than an index - a list that shifted between reading it and acting on it would
# otherwise delete a different backup than the one somebody chose.
method DeleteBackup(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Absolute path, exactly as Backups reported it.
  bundle: string,
  # Reports what would go, having removed nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # DELETED, PREVIEWED, NO_SUCH_BACKUP or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # Whether the file itself was there to remove. False with DELETED means the record was cleared
  # for a bundle somebody had already moved - which is a tidy-up, not a loss.
  fileRemoved: bool,
  # How many pushes it held, from the record. What a person is giving up.
  refs: int,
  detail: string
)

# Copies a credential an agent already holds on this machine into the vault.
#
# The one credential operation that carries no secret over this socket in either direction: the
# daemon reads a file on its own disk and writes its own vault, and all that crosses is an agent's
# name. So it is unaffected by whether storing a typed secret is ever allowed.
#
# It is the better first offer wherever an agent can log itself in, because nothing has to be
# retyped - and the commonest typo when retyping is storing the guide's placeholder.
method ImportCredential(
  # Which agent to import from. Omit when only one is installed.
  agent: ?string,
  # Where it keeps its credentials. Omit for what the agent declares.
  configDirectory: ?string
) -> (
  # IMPORTED, NO_SUCH_AGENT, NO_CONFIG_DIRECTORY, NOTHING_TO_IMPORT, VAULT_LOCKED or FAILED.
  #
  # NOTHING_TO_IMPORT is the ordinary case rather than a fault: the agent is installed and nobody
  # has logged in with it here yet. VAULT_LOCKED is not a missing credential and must not be shown
  # as one - it is "we cannot tell you until you unlock it at the machine".
  outcome: string,
  # The vault key it was stored under. "" when nothing was stored.
  name: string,
  # The kind, as the agent recorded it. "" when nothing was stored.
  type: string,
  # How many characters arrived, so somebody can see that it worked without seeing what worked.
  # Never the value. 0 when nothing was stored.
  length: int,
  # The file it was read from, on the daemon's machine. "" when nothing was stored.
  source: string,
  # Why it failed, or a warning about what was stored - a credential that looks like a
  # placeholder, or one a task will refuse. "" when there is nothing to say.
  detail: string
)

# Which model providers this machine has, and which of them work could start against.
#
# The read half of authentication, and it carries no secret: names, kinds and whether something is
# stored, never a value.
#
# Cheap in the ordinary case. It consults the installed agents only when the vault holds a name
# that is not a provider's - the one situation where the key below can be an agent's name - so a
# vault written by a current Sokar costs nothing extra.
method Providers() -> (
  providers: []Provider,
  # Whether the vault could be read at all. False means locked or undecryptable, and then
  # 'authenticated' below is unknown rather than false - the same distinction Credentials draws,
  # for the same reason: "we cannot tell you until you unlock it" and "there is none" send a
  # person to different places.
  readable: bool
)

type Provider (
  # Short name, as an agent names it and as 'vault put' takes it.
  name: string,
  # Human-readable label.
  label: string,
  # Where requests are forwarded to.
  upstream: string,
  # Wire formats it serves, the provider's own first.
  dialects: []string,
  # Whether the vault holds a credential for it. MEANINGLESS unless 'readable' - render it only
  # beside that.
  authenticated: bool,
  # The kind stored: "api-key", "oauth", or "" when none is stored or none was recorded.
  credentialType: string,
  # The name a credential for this provider is stored under. Usually the provider's own name -
  # but a vault written before credentials were keyed by provider answers under the AGENT's name,
  # and that key stays in use so upgrading does not stop anyone authenticating.
  #
  # Computed here because a client cannot: intersecting "providers" with "stored names" reports a
  # missing credential for precisely the vault that has one, since the fallback key is invisible
  # from outside.
  credentialName: string,
  # The exact line to run at the machine to store one, ready to render verbatim. Always with the
  # provider's name, even when 'credentialName' is still an agent's: that is where it belongs, and
  # a task finds it there first. A subscription
  # token rather than an API key needs '--type oauth' on the end - they go in different headers,
  # and sending one as the other fails as an authentication error that looks like a wrong key.
  storeCommand: string
)

# Whether this machine can actually run a task.
#
# Every dependency below fails far from its cause - without nft a container comes up with no
# ruleset, without nsenter a clearance decision cannot reach a running task - so today a person
# learns about them by starting work and watching it behave strangely. This is the question they
# cannot otherwise ask: is this machine able to do the thing I am about to ask of it.
#
# It runs external programs to find out, so it takes a moment and is not something to poll. It is
# still a read: nothing here changes anything.
method Doctor() -> (
  probes: []Probe,
  # False when anything is MISSING. Not a summary a client should re-derive: this is the same rule
  # the CLI exits non-zero on, so the two cannot come to different conclusions about one machine.
  #
  # DEGRADED and UNKNOWN leave this true. The machine runs tasks, and the probes say how well.
  ready: bool
)

# One thing outside Sokar that a task depends on.
type Probe (
  # What is being reported, as an operator would name it.
  name: string,
  # OK, DEGRADED, MISSING or UNKNOWN. UNKNOWN is its own state rather than the good case: a probe
  # that guesses well is indistinguishable from one that works.
  state: string,
  # What was found, in one line.
  detail: string,
  # The single next action. "" only when the state is OK - a probe that fails and names nothing to
  # do about it cannot be constructed on this side, so an interface may render this without
  # checking whether it is there.
  action: string
)

method Node() -> (
  # Stable across restarts, and unique to this node. Random, and derived from nothing: it says
  # only whether two connections lead to the same place.
  #
  # A node is an OS user with a daemon, not a machine, so two accounts on one host answer
  # differently here - which is the case a hostname gets wrong.
  id: string
)

# Which logs a task has.
#
# Ask rather than assume: what a task writes depends on what it started - a task with no gate
# has no gate.log, one run with --clearance off has no clearance.log - so a client holding a
# list of names would show an empty viewer for a file that was never going to exist, and would
# never show one a later release adds. Empty for a task whose state directory is gone, which is
# what removal does.
method Logs(task: string) -> (logs: []Log)

# What a task holds that never reached the gate.
#
# Asked for ONE task, never on a listing. Every other field about a task comes from one 'ps' and
# the state directory; this one runs git inside the container, which on a list a client redraws
# would be a call per row - the cost 'since' and 'behindMeasured' both exist to avoid.
#
# 'readable' is not "holds nothing". Holding nothing is readable with two zeros. Unreadable means
# nobody could look: a task killed, or stopped by a Sokar that left no note. Absence rendering as
# nothing-to-worry-about is the failure this separation exists to prevent - the same one that put
# 'readable' on Credentials and Providers.
#
# 'asOf' is absent while the task runs, because the answer is current. For a stopped task it is
# when the answer was true: the workspace lives inside the container, so once it stops the only
# source is what the stop wrote down.
method WorkHeld(
  # Container name.
  task: string
) -> (
  # Whether what it holds could be established at all.
  readable: bool,
  # Files changed and not committed.
  changedFiles: int,
  # Commits on no remote.
  unpushedCommits: int,
  # When this was true, ISO-8601. Absent while the task runs.
  asOf: ?string
)

method Tail(
  # Container name.
  task: string,
  # File name within the task's state directory, ending in .log. Not a path.
  log: string
) -> (lines: []string)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- the gate

# What is waiting at a project's gate.
#
# The gate methods take a project NAME rather than a task, because a gate belongs to a project and
# one daemon serves every project the operator has.
#
# A NAME and not a path: a client cannot see this machine's filesystem and has no path to invent,
# and which project a call is about must not depend on a directory. The machine resolves it, and
# prefers the file it verified - so a call and a task cannot end up reading two different files.
#
# Each of them also takes 'repository', because a gate belongs to ONE REPOSITORY of that project:
# every repository has its own mirror, its own review branch and its own answer to "what is waiting
# for review". Omitting it means the project's own repository - the one the project file belongs
# to - which is the only default in this interface that is not Sokar picking between equals.
# Project.repositories lists the names.
method Pending(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Upstream to gate against. Default: the project's own.
  upstream: ?string,
  # Which repository of the project. Default: the project's own.
  repository: ?string
) -> (
  # Path to the bare mirror the agent pushes into.
  mirror: string,
  # offline, gatekeeping or online.
  mode: string,
  # Where the mirror was seeded from, or "" when it was not.
  seededFrom: string,
  pending: []PendingPush
)

# What a pending push contains, so it can be judged before it is forwarded.
method Review(
  project: string,
  upstream: ?string,
  # Which repository of the project. Default: the project's own.
  repository: ?string,
  # Ref name, from PendingPush.name.
  name: string,
  # What to diff against. Default: the upstream's default branch.
  against: ?string
) -> (
  # Unified diff, in the order of files: the dangerous first, reformatting and generated files last.
  diff: string,
  # Commit log over the same range.
  log: string,
  # What the push changes, ranked; the diff shows them in this order.
  files: []ReviewFile,
  # What the task that pushed this was asked, to show before the files - never compared with them.
  # "" when it was started without an instruction; absent when the task is gone from this machine.
  asked: ?string
)

# Forwards a pending push upstream. The only call in this interface that sends anything
# anywhere, and it makes the caller name the branch rather than inferring one.
method Approve(
  project: string,
  upstream: ?string,
  # Which repository of the project. Default: the project's own.
  repository: ?string,
  name: string,
  # Branch to push to. Required.
  branch: string
) -> (forwarded: string, branch: string)

# Drops a pending push. The work stays in the mirror; only the request is gone.
method Reject(project: string, upstream: ?string, repository: ?string, name: string)
    -> (rejected: string)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- clearance

# Blocked connections from every running task, as they happen.
#
# Streaming only: answering this once would look like it worked and then deliver nothing ever
# again, so a call without "more" is refused.
#
# One subscription covers every task, including tasks started after the call - an interface
# does not discover and connect to each task's own socket.
method Prompts() -> Prompt

# Answers one prompt.
#
# A task that is not running has no watcher to tell, and that is refused rather than accepted
# and dropped: an answer that goes nowhere leaves the operator believing they unblocked
# something.
method Decide(
  # Container name, from Prompt.task.
  task: string,
  # From Prompt.key, unchanged.
  key: string,
  # From Prompt.destination.
  address: string,
  # Whether to let it through.
  allow: ?bool
) -> (ok: bool)

# One repository of a project, and the state that belongs to it rather than to the project.
#
# A project is a unit of work over one or more repositories and a task works on exactly one of
# them, so each of these has its own mirror, its own review branch and its own distance from its
# own upstream. A screen that showed one of each per PROJECT would be showing one repository's
# numbers against all of them.
type Repository (
  # What Start and the gate methods take as 'repository'.
  name: string,
  # Whether this is the project's own repository - where project.yml, the planning and the issues
  # live. Reported rather than left to be worked out by comparing this to the project's name.
  own: bool,
  # Where approved work goes, or "" when it has none. A repository without an upstream is one
  # whose work stays on this machine, which is a legitimate state.
  upstream: string,
  # The bare mirror on this machine, or "" when it has never been used.
  mirror: string,
  # Pushes waiting for review in that mirror. The project's own 'pending' is the sum of these.
  pending: int,
  # Commits this repository's upstream has that its mirror does not. MEANINGLESS unless
  # behindReason is MEASURED, exactly as on the project. Never render it without behindMeasured.
  behind: int,
  # When 'behind' was measured, RFC 3339. "" when it never was.
  behindMeasured: string,
  # Why 'behind' is what it is. Same values as the project's.
  behindReason: string,
  behindDetail: string,
  # Everything a task on this repository may reach, each saying which block granted it.
  #
  # ONE list with a source per entry, not two to subtract from one another: a repository's grants
  # are ADDED to the project's, and a screen showing both halves should not have to work out which
  # is which.
  egress: []Grant,
  # What a task on this repository may consume, and where each key came from.
  limits: ResolvedLimits
)

# One thing a repository may reach.
type Grant (
  # The set name or the host.
  value: string,
  # "set" or "domain".
  kind: string,
  # "project" or "repository" - which block declared it.
  from: string
)

# What a task on one repository may consume, resolved.
#
# A repository's limits REPLACE the project's key by key, so the interesting part is which key it
# replaced. The three '...From' fields say "repository" when that repository declared the key and
# "project" otherwise - and "project" covers BOTH a value the project file wrote and Sokar's own
# default, which this side cannot tell apart today. Do not render "project" as "written by hand".
type ResolvedLimits (
  # Memory limit, or "" for no limit.
  memory: string,
  # CPU limit, or "" for no limit.
  cpus: string,
  # Process limit.
  pids: int,
  memoryFrom: string,
  cpusFrom: string,
  pidsFrom: string
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- following a repository

# Which project repositories this account takes its configuration from.
#
# Per account and not per machine: two people here follow different projects, reconcile at different
# moments, and each reaches their own vault for the credential a private repository needs.
method Following() -> (projects: []Followed)

type Followed (
  name: string,
  # Where its repository is.
  url: string,
  # The last commit this machine verified and applied, or "" when none ever was. WHAT IS RUNNING.
  commit: string,
  # When it last tried, RFC 3339, or "".
  at: string,
  # Why this project is where it is. "" when it never tried. New values may appear; render an
  # unrecognised one rather than throwing.
  #
  #   APPLIED       the commit below is in force
  #   UNCHANGED     it already held that commit
  #   NOT_SIGNED    the fetched commit carries no signature. Usually somebody forgot to sign
  #   UNKNOWN_KEY   a GOOD signature by a key this machine was never given. Either a key that has
  #                 moved or somebody putting a project file past the machine - this is the one
  #                 that belongs in front of a person as a possible attempt
  #   NO_ANCHOR     nothing is pinned here, so nothing can be verified
  #   UNREADABLE    the commit or the repository could not be read
  #   REWRITTEN     verified, and not a descendant of what is in force. Waits for acceptRewrite
  #   UNREACHABLE   the repository could not be reached. Look at the network or the URL
  #   VAULT_LOCKED  this account's vault is shut, so the credential a private repository needs is
  #                 out of reach. NOT the same as UNREACHABLE: the action is to unlock, and
  #                 nothing is wrong with the network or the URL
  #   UNUSABLE      signed, and project.yml does not read as a project
  #   FAILED        something else, said in detail
  outcome: string,
  # The commit that was TURNED AWAY, or "" when nothing was.
  #
  # Never the same field as 'commit': what is running and what was rejected are different
  # questions, and a screen asks both. A project goes on running what it had.
  refused: string,
  # The fingerprint of the key that signed the refused commit, or "" when there is none to name.
  #
  # Only the fingerprint, which is not a secret - it is what lets a person compare it with the key
  # they meant to pin. Nothing else about the key is said, and nothing from the vault ever is.
  signer: string,
  # Whether nothing will change about this project until somebody acts.
  #
  # NOT the same as "it failed": an unreachable repository may answer on the next pass by itself,
  # a refused signature never will. Derived from 'outcome' on this side, so a client never has to
  # keep a list of which outcomes are stuck in step with ours - read this, not the outcome.
  needsAPerson: bool,
  # Whether this project is followed WITHOUT an anchor: what its repository says is applied with
  # no signature checked at all.
  #
  # A state, not an error - somebody asked for it. But show it wherever this project is: without a
  # signature the rule is "whoever may push there decides what tasks here may reach", rather than
  # "whoever holds the signing key". It is per project; one unverified project says nothing about
  # another.
  unverified: bool,
  detail: string
)

# Starts following a project's repository, and reconciles once, now.
#
# A repository URL is not a secret, so it may travel over this socket - unlike a credential, which
# never does. Following the same project at the same address again is not an error; the same name at
# a different address is refused, because that is a different project wearing a name.
method Follow(
  name: string,
  url: string,
  # The public key this project's configuration is signed with, as "ssh-ed25519 AAAA...".
  #
  # Pins it for this project and follows in one command. The key must come from the PERSON - a
  # colleague, a wiki, the team lead - and NEVER out of the repository it verifies: an anchor that
  # travels with the thing it authenticates authenticates nothing.
  #
  # A FINGERPRINT is accepted here too, as "SHA256:...". That is the string a refusal names, so it
  # is the one a person has in front of them. The key itself is then read out of the commit that
  # was turned away - and only once its fingerprint is the one they typed, which is what keeps the
  # anchor coming from the person. A commit signed by any other key is refused and nothing is
  # pinned.
  signedBy: ?string,
  # Follows without an anchor. Nothing is checked, and 'unverified' says so from then on.
  #
  # Mutually exclusive with signedBy, which is refused rather than one of them quietly winning.
  unverified: ?bool,
  # Accepts a history rewrite: forgets the commit in force, so the next reconcile has nothing to
  # descend from and applies what it verifies.
  #
  # ONLY in answer to REWRITTEN, and only when a person has said they mean it. A properly signed
  # commit that is not a descendant of the one in force is either somebody with the key having
  # rebased, or somebody WITHOUT it re-serving an older signed configuration to put back a rule
  # that was taken away - which needs no key at all, only control of the route. From here the two
  # are indistinguishable, so this must never be sent automatically or as a retry.
  acceptRewrite: ?bool,
  # Asks whether following this would work, and records NOTHING.
  #
  # Everything a real follow does, against a scratch clone that is deleted afterwards: it fetches
  # with the credential this machine would use, verifies the signature, reads the file and checks
  # the egress sets. The answer is READY, or the same named reason the real follow would give -
  # so a dialog can offer 'Follow it' only when it would work, and put the way out of each refusal
  # beside it. Nothing this account follows moves, and no project is created.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # READY (only with dryRun), APPLIED, UNCHANGED, REWRITTEN, UNREACHABLE, VAULT_LOCKED,
  # NO_CREDENTIAL, UNUSABLE, NOT_SIGNED, UNKNOWN_KEY, NO_ANCHOR, UNREADABLE or FAILED.
  #
  # A project this account was NOT already following and that does not end in APPLIED or UNCHANGED
  # is NOT recorded: the refusal is the whole answer and the machine is as it was. 'recorded' says
  # so. A project it was already following keeps its record whatever happens, because that record
  # is where the reason lives - see Following().
  #
  # REWRITTEN is the one that needs a person: 'detail' says what was found, and the only way past
  # it is calling again with acceptRewrite. Do not treat it as a transient failure.
  #
  # The full set is Followed.outcome's.
  outcome: string,
  commit: string,
  detail: string,
  # The commit turned away, or "". Never the same as 'commit'.
  refused: string,
  # With UNKNOWN_HOST_KEY and HOST_KEY_CHANGED: the host, spelled exactly as TrustHostKey wants
  # it, so a client passes it back unchanged. Answered rather than left to be read off the address
  # again - 'user@host:path', a port, and an alias in somebody's ssh config are three readings of
  # one thing. "" otherwise.
  #
  # It is the host the ADDRESS names. An alias in ~/.ssh/config that points somewhere else is
  # resolved by ssh and not by this, so on a machine that uses one, what ssh records and what this
  # reports can differ - the one case where this name is not the last word.
  host: string,
  # With UNKNOWN_HOST_KEY and HOST_KEY_CHANGED: what that host offers right now. A person cannot
  # confirm a fingerprint they have not been shown, and sending them to another call to see it
  # would be making them fetch what this already has. Empty otherwise.
  hostKeys: []HostKey,
  # What to type on that machine to store the credential a follow needs, with NO_CREDENTIAL, and
  # "" otherwise. Named by the machine: which vault entry a follow reads is the machine's
  # knowledge, and the secret itself must never travel over this socket.
  storeCommand: string,
  # Whether anything was written. False for a dry run, and false for a first follow that was
  # refused - nothing of that project is here.
  recorded: bool,
  # The fingerprint of the key that signed it, or "".
  signer: string,
  # Whether nothing will change until somebody acts. NOT the same as "it failed": an unreachable
  # repository may answer on the next pass by itself, a refused signature never will.
  needsAPerson: bool
)

# Stops following, and removes the project.
#
# It deletes, so it refuses first: a mirror may hold pushes nobody has reviewed and tasks may still
# be up. The same refusal DeleteProject gives, from the same code.
#
# It answers for every project this machine LISTS, not only the ones it follows. A project made
# before following existed - a task was started with a file, which wrote a mirror and a registry
# entry - is in no follow record, and it used to get NoSuchProject here: an interface could offer
# to clear it away and have nothing to show for it, while force would have swept it blind.
# NoSuchProject now means what it says: this machine does not list that project at all.
method Unfollow(
  name: string,
  # Says what would go, and removes nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool,
  # Removes it although work is waiting for review. That work is destroyed.
  force: ?bool
) -> (
  # DELETED, PREVIEWED, HOLDS_WORK, TASKS_RUNNING or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # Refs nobody reviewed, with HOLDS_WORK and with force - it is what force destroys.
  unreviewed: []string,
  # Tasks that are up, with TASKS_RUNNING.
  running: []string,
  # What goes: the mirrors, the task image, the build directory, the registry entry, the recorded
  # distance, the followed clone and every task. Filled for a REFUSAL too, so the cost stands
  # beside the reason.
  removes: []Removal,
  # What is deliberately NOT touched - the real upstream above all. Named so a confirmation never
  # lists the operator's own things among the casualties.
  keeps: []string,
  # Whether this account was following it at all. False for a project that predates following:
  # there is a mirror and an image to clear away, and no repository to stop taking from. A
  # confirmation says "stop following and remove" or just "remove" from this.
  following: bool,
  detail: string
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- keyslots

# What can open this machine's vault.
#
# The vault's master key is stored only in wrapped form, once per credential allowed to open it.
# Keyslot 0 is the passphrase and is recovery only; every other slot is one device, which keeps a
# 32-byte share in its own platform's keystore. Adding a device adds a slot, removing one deletes a
# slot, and neither re-keys anything nor disturbs another device.
#
# Readable while the vault is locked: a screen has to show which devices can open a vault before
# anything opens it. Nothing here is secret.
method Keyslots() -> (slots: []Keyslot)

type Keyslot (
  # Assigned by the node and kept by the device beside its share. Not derived from the share: the
  # node discards that at enrollment and could derive nothing from it later.
  id: string,
  # What a person called this device. Shown in a list; never an identifier.
  name: string,
  # USER_SCOPED, APPLICATION_SCOPED, FIDO2 or TPM2, as the device declared it. Recorded, not
  # verified - this machine cannot check what another one does with its own keystore.
  storage: string,
  enrolled: string,
  # "" when it has not been used since it was enrolled.
  lastUsed: string,
  # True only for the slot whose share this machine is currently holding, so a list can mark
  # "this device". About the session, not about the file.
  self: bool,
  # True for the passphrase, which is keyslot 0, is recovery only, and is not a device.
  recovery: bool
)

# Lets a device open the vault from now on.
#
# The share arrives once, wraps the master key, and is discarded. It is never logged, never echoed
# back and never written anywhere. What is left here is worth nothing without the device, and what
# the device holds is worth nothing without this machine's vault file.
method EnrollDevice(
  # What to call the device in a list.
  name: string,
  # 32 random bytes, base64. The device generates them and keeps them in its platform's keystore.
  share: string,
  # USER_SCOPED, APPLICATION_SCOPED, FIDO2 or TPM2.
  storage: string
) -> (
  # ENROLLED, ALREADY_ENROLLED, UNKNOWN_STORAGE, BAD_SHARE, VAULT_LOCKED,
  # VAULT_WITHOUT_KEYSLOTS or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  slot: ?Keyslot,
  detail: string
)

# Takes a device's way in away.
#
# Deleting one wrapped blob and nothing else: no re-keying, no other device disturbed, no
# passphrase rotated.
method RevokeKeyslot(id: string) -> (
  # REVOKED, NO_SUCH_SLOT, LAST_WAY_IN, VAULT_LOCKED, VAULT_WITHOUT_KEYSLOTS or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # What can still open the vault afterwards, so a screen can say it without asking again.
  remaining: []Keyslot,
  detail: string
)

# Opens the vault with a device's share, for a while.
#
# This is the one thing the daemon can do to the vault that it cannot do with a passphrase: a share
# arrives from a device over this socket, where a passphrase would need a terminal the daemon does
# not have.
#
# What is held afterwards is the share, in the kernel keyring, with the kernel enforcing the
# timeout - not the unwrapped master key, which would be a secret on this machine that outlives
# every revocation.
method UnlockWithShare(
  # The device's share, base64.
  share: string,
  # How long, in minutes. Absent means eight hours.
  minutes: ?int
) -> (
  # UNLOCKED, SHARE_REJECTED, BAD_SHARE, VAULT_WITHOUT_KEYSLOTS or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # When the unlock runs out, RFC 3339, or "".
  until: string,
  slot: ?Keyslot,
  detail: string
)

# What this machine could install, from its own package source.
#
# Nobody maintains a list: every agent package declares 'Provides: sokar-agent' and every transport
# 'Provides: sokar-transport', so the repository is the catalogue and a package published today is
# offered today. The names here are always real packages, never the virtual one - a virtual package
# with several providers cannot be installed by its own name.
#
# The message filter is deliberately absent. It is not one of a kind to choose from: it is the one
# thing without which nothing leaves, and it is always installed.
#
# As fresh as the machine's last index refresh, which needs root and is not done here.
method Installable() -> (packages: []Installable)

type Installable (
  # The real package name, which is what may be installed.
  name: string,
  # 'agent' or 'transport'. What a person chooses between - the virtual package name that this
  # was read from ('sokar-agent', 'sokar-transport') is the mechanism and is not returned.
  kind: string,
  # One line, from the package itself.
  description: string,
  # Whether it is on this machine now.
  installed: bool,
  # What is installed, or what the repository offers. "" when neither is known.
  version: string
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- messages

# The peers this task may address, and what a person decided about each.
#
# Two things at once because they are read together: the project says who may be addressed, and
# the mailbox says whether anything currently goes there. An interface that showed only the first
# would show a peer as available while every message to it waits.
method Peers(
  # Container name of the task.
  task: string,
  # The project's name, as the other project methods take it. Its verified project.yml is where the peers are declared.
  project: string
) -> (peers: []TalkPeer)

type TalkPeer (
  # What a message addresses.
  name: string,
  # '<transport>:<rest>'.
  address: string,
  # 'vouched' or 'external'. It decides whether what arrives is checked again here.
  trust: string,
  # How many messages a day this task may exchange with it, each way.
  perDay: int,
  # How many went out to it in the last day, as the budget counts them: a rolling day, so
  # nothing is released by midnight arriving. The next one out is refused at perDay.
  sentToday: int,
  # How many from it reached the agent in the last day, counted the same way.
  receivedToday: int,
  # 'prompt', 'allow', 'deny' or 'off' - clearance's four, with the same meanings.
  mode: string,
  # Whether everything for this peer waits, whatever the mode says. Mode and held are the project's:
  # every task of it, including tasks started later, sees the same.
  held: bool
)

# Every mailbox on this machine, as it moves. Streaming only, as Prompts is.
#
# The events come from each task's record, which is written as things happen. There is no second
# copy of the conversation to disagree with it, and an entry never carries what a message said -
# a stream that quoted refused messages would be a second copy of exactly what was refused.
#
# Live only: it says what happens from the moment a client listens, and replays nothing. What is
# already waiting for a person when a client starts is Held.
method Talk() -> TalkEvent

# Every message a person may be asked to decide about, as it stands now - for an interface that
# starts after a message was held, which the live stream alone would show as "nothing needs you".
#
# Found exactly as ReadHeld and Release find a message, so the list, the reading and the decision
# cannot disagree. Without what the messages say: reading one is a person's deliberate act, through
# ReadHeld. held and refused-by-filter wait for a decision; refused-by-person and unchecked are
# listed because they are still there, and neither can be sent.
method Held(
  # Container name of one task, or absent for every task on this machine.
  task: ?string
) -> (messages: []HeldMessage)

type HeldMessage (
  # Container name of the task whose mailbox holds it.
  task: string,
  # held, refused-by-filter, refused-by-person or unchecked.
  standing: string,
  # The file, as ReadHeld and Release take it.
  message: string,
  # The message id, or "" when none could be read.
  id: string,
  # ROLE_USER when a person wrote it, ROLE_AGENT when a task did, or "".
  role: string,
  # The peer it was going to or came from, or "".
  peer: string,
  # question, answer, status, review-request or handover, or "".
  kind: string,
  # When this host first recorded it, RFC 3339, or "".
  at: string,
  # Why it is held, or, for a refused one, the filter's own answer.
  reason: string,
  # "out" when this task was sending it, "in" when it was reaching this task - an external peer's
  # message is held on its way in, before any agent sees it. "" for a message held before the
  # record kept directions.
  direction: string
)

type TalkEvent (
  # Container name of the task whose mailbox this is about.
  task: string,
  # When it happened, RFC 3339.
  at: string,
  # taken, queued, sent, deferred, held, delivered, duplicate, refused (by a person), overridden
  # (delivered by a person although the filter refused it) or read (what the task sent was read where it
  # went, as a transport that confirms reading says; detail gives when).
  event: string,
  # The message's file name.
  message: string,
  # Its message id, or "" when none could be read.
  id: string,
  # The peer it was going to or came from, or "".
  peer: string,
  # Why, for the events that have a why. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

# Writes a person's own message into a conversation.
#
# It lands in the outbox and goes out through the filter like any other message: a person is not a
# reason to skip the check. What differs is the role, which says a person wrote it.
method Say(
  # Container name of the task.
  task: string,
  # Who it is addressed to.
  peer: string,
  # question, answer, status, review-request or handover. Empty means question.
  kind: string,
  # What it says.
  text: string,
  # The conversation it belongs to, or empty for a new one.
  context: ?string
) -> (
  # The file written into the outbox.
  message: string,
  # WRITTEN. Not 'sent': the next pass decides that.
  outcome: string
)

# Reads one message a person is asked to decide about, in full, so they decide about what it says
# rather than releasing it unread: a held one, one the filter refused, or one it could not check.
#
# Looked up exactly as Release looks it up. A refused message holds what was refused, secrets
# included, and is shown to the person asking - at the operator's word, so a person can still decide
# to deliver it. It is never in the stream or the record, which stay without content.
method ReadHeld(
  # Container name of the task.
  task: string,
  # Message id or file name, as Talk, Release and 'sokar talk held' take it.
  id: string
) -> (
  # FOUND, NO_SUCH_MESSAGE or AMBIGUOUS.
  outcome: string,
  # held, refused-by-filter, refused-by-person or unchecked, or "". What Release can do with it follows
  # from this: held and refused-by-filter can be sent, the other two never.
  standing: string,
  # The file, or "".
  message: string,
  # The message id, or "".
  id: string,
  # ROLE_USER when a person wrote it, ROLE_AGENT when a task did, or "".
  role: string,
  # The peer it was going to or came from, or "".
  peer: string,
  # question, answer, status, review-request or handover, or "".
  kind: string,
  # When this host first recorded it, RFC 3339, or "".
  at: string,
  # Its parts, in order, exactly as written: render them as text and never interpret them - no
  # markup, no link resolved, nothing fetched. A part that is not text is named by its media type.
  text: []string,
  # Why it is held, or, for a refused one, the filter's own answer - which rules it broke.
  reason: string,
  # "out" when this task was sending it, "in" when it was reaching this task - an external peer's
  # message is held on its way in, before any agent sees it. "" for a message held before the
  # record kept directions.
  direction: string
)

# Releases one held message, or refuses it for good - or, for one the filter refused, delivers it
# after all, or keeps it refused as a person's decision.
#
# Released or refused, never edited: what the record shows a task said has to be what the task
# said. A refused message delivered after all goes straight to its transport, never through the
# filter's accepted directory, and the record says it went despite the filter. An external peer's
# own filter checks it again. What a person refused for good, and what the filter could not check,
# is never sent.
method Release(
  # Container name of the task.
  task: string,
  # Message id or file name, as Talk and 'sokar talk held' show it.
  id: string,
  # Whether to refuse it for good. The sender is told.
  refuse: ?bool
) -> (
  # RELEASED, REFUSED, DELIVERED_DESPITE_FILTER, NOT_DELIVERABLE, NO_SUCH_MESSAGE or AMBIGUOUS.
  outcome: string,
  # The file it acted on, or "".
  message: string,
  # The message id, or "".
  id: string,
  # Why, for NOT_DELIVERABLE. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

# Holds a peer, releases it, or changes how much is asked before a message reaches it.
#
# For the whole project: every task of it, including tasks started later, follows what is decided here.
#
# 'allow' and 'off' mean work nobody has read leaves the machine. In a project that is not
# 'online' that is refused unless the project carries 'unread_work_may_leave: true' - the same
# setting asked before unreviewed work goes to a review branch.
method Moderate(
  # Ignored: a decision is the project's, not one task's. Accepted so an older client still works.
  task: ?string,
  # The project's name, as the other project methods take it. Its verified project.yml decides whether unread work may leave.
  project: string,
  # The peer to decide about.
  name: string,
  # Whether to hold everything for it, or absent to leave that as it is.
  held: ?bool,
  # The new mode, or absent to leave it as it is.
  mode: ?string
) -> (name: string, mode: string, held: bool)


# Lets a person into a project's conversation with an account made for them, or gives it a new password
# with reset. Answered once: the login is in this reply and nowhere else, never kept.
#
# 'login' is the transport's, passed through as it gave it. For Matrix: homeserver, user, room, password,
# and loopback ("true" when the homeserver is this machine's loopback) with port - a client elsewhere then
# forwards that port (ssh -L port:127.0.0.1:port) before its Matrix client reaches the homeserver. 'shown'
# is what a terminal prints.
method JoinMessages(
  project: string,
  # Whom the account is for, as MessageMembers lists them.
  person: string,
  # Give the person's existing account a new password; without it, a second join is MemberExists.
  reset: ?bool
) -> (
  transport: string,
  login: [string]string,
  shown: string
)

# Who has joined a project's conversation, by the name join was given.
method MessageMembers(project: string) -> (members: []MessageMember)

# The person has joined already; reset gives them a new password.
error MemberExists(person: string, user: string)

# The project has no conversation: no peer is reached through a transport's own ('<scheme>:').
error NoConversation(project: string)

# The transport refused; message is its own words, code its exit code (75 temporary, 77 refused, 78
# configuration missing).
error ConversationRefused(message: string, code: int)

# Grants an authorization once, for this account's later tasks. Streaming only.
#
# The first reply is state "needed", with the link to open, whole. For a device code (kind oauth-device) it
# carries the code to type if the link does not, and the person decides in any browser anywhere. For a
# redirect (kind oauth-code) the code is empty and port is the loopback port on the machine the answer comes
# back to: a client whose browser is elsewhere forwards that port (ssh -L port:127.0.0.1:port) before opening
# the link. The last reply is "granted", "refused", "expired" or "failed" (with a detail). The grant is kept
# in the account's vault, hidden, with who granted it and when; no task holds it.
method Authorize(
  # The vault entry of kind oauth-device or oauth-code that names the service.
  name: string
) -> (state: string, link: string, code: string, port: int, expiresIn: int, detail: string)

# Every destination declared on this machine, the one in force for each name first.
method Destinations() -> (destinations: []Destination)

# One destination, as it is in force.
method Destination(name: string) -> (destination: Destination)

# Declares a destination in the user's own directory, taking the place of a packaged one of the same name.
#
# With dryRun, nothing is written and the answer says what would be: the destination as it would be read
# back, or the refusal - a name that is not one, an upstream that is not https, no header.
method WriteDestination(
  name: string,
  label: ?string,
  upstream: string,
  # Default: "Authorization".
  authHeader: ?string,
  authPrefix: ?string,
  authQuery: ?string,
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (destination: Destination, written: bool)

# Removes the user's own declaration of a destination. A packaged one cannot be removed; if one of the same
# name exists, it is in force again afterwards.
method RemoveDestination(name: string) -> (removed: bool, file: string)

# The name is not a destination declared on this machine. From Destination, 'name' is the destination asked
# for. From Start, it is the CREDENTIAL whose destination is not declared - the entry the project or the run
# named, which is what the person corrects - and never the destination's name.
error NoSuchDestination(name: string)

# The destination as given is not one Sokar can use; the message says why.
error DestinationRefused(message: string)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- errors

# The name is not a task Sokar created.
#
# An error rather than an empty-looking answer, deliberately. WorkHeld answers "readable: false"
# for a task nobody could look inside, and a name that was never a task is not that - conflating
# them would let a client's wrong argument arrive as a legitimate answer, which is the failure
# the Backups documentation gap produced.
error NoSuchTask(task: string)

# The named log is not a file in that task's state directory.
error NoSuchLog(task: string, log: string)

# The method only works as a stream and was called without "more".
error StreamRequired(method: string)

# The task is not running, so nothing can act on the answer.
error NoClearance(task: string)

# A gate method was called without naming a project file.
# A rebuild depth this daemon does not know.
#
# Refused rather than treated as the default: quietly doing the cheapest thing when somebody asked
# for the most expensive is exactly the failure the depths exist to prevent.
error UnknownRebuild(rebuild: string)

# This account does not follow a project of that name.
#
# An error rather than an empty answer, for the same reason NoSuchTask is one: a caller that asked
# about something that is not there has a wrong argument, and reporting it as "nothing to do" lets
# that wrong argument arrive as a legitimate result.
error NoSuchProject(project: string)

error ProjectRequired()

# WidenTask was called without saying how far the change goes.
error ScopeRequired()

# Approve was called without a branch to push to.
error BranchRequired(name: string)

# Anything else that went wrong. The message is for a person, not for a client to match on.
error Failed(message: string)
